#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Alert while an agent box is still able to act, when its CPU or its swap runs out.

Why this exists: on 2026-10-29 between about 22:46Z and 23:58Z, six agent lanes on
ossv-agent-open (c-asan-rt, c-libc-str, g-ph-hreply, c-omx-writer, c-asanb, h-cmd-b2) were
reported frozen and the box was read as hung. It was not hung. Measured at 23:55-23:58Z: 32
cores carrying load 251/185/135, `cpu some avg60` at 80%, 126 `omp` processes holding 108 GiB
of RSS and 180 GiB of swap, and `/swapfile` at 200,820,180 of 201,326,588 KB used -- 99.75%,
515 MB free. `ossv-worker list` showed 115 workers in `running` state. Nothing alerted. Every
liveness check a person could run said the box was healthy: omp was running in every pane,
`tmux capture-pane` answered in 0.01 s, no process was stuck in a syscall, and the transcripts
kept being written. The lanes were simply being served a fraction of a core each, with their
working sets in a swapfile that had no room left.

That failure mode is invisible to the two watches already on these boxes. `disk-fill-watch`
watches bytes on a filesystem. `omp-memory-watch` watches one session's own memcg against the
ceiling `shims/omp` gave it, which is a per-session limit and says nothing about a box carrying
a hundred of them. The box-wide resource that ran out here -- CPU time, and swap -- had no
instrument at all.

What one run does, from /proc only, with no model call and no subprocess on the quiet path:

  1. Record one sample of CPU pressure, swap, memory and the run queue. Every run appends,
     whether or not anything is wrong: a silent log cannot be told from a dead watch.
  2. Judge each condition on its own, and give each its own verdict line. CPU and swap are
     different states with different actions, and one headline for both overstates whichever
     half has recovered: on 2026-10-30T01:32Z an escalation said "STILL OUT OF CPU+SWAP" about a
     box carrying load 27 on 32 cores at 7% CPU pressure, because the CPU half had been true
     half an hour earlier. Each condition has its own crossing, grace, alert, new-fact check and
     clear, and a line in a message only ever says what is true of its condition in that run.
     The lines of one run go in ONE message: on 2026-11-01T16:36Z CPU and SWAP went out as two
     5.6-6 KB messages in the same minute with the same load line and the same consumer table.
  3. The CPU rule asks two things, because either alone is wrong. PSI (`cpu some avg60`, time
     tasks spent runnable and waiting for a CPU) must be at or over CPU_STALL, AND the load
     must be over the core count. Load alone counts uninterruptible sleep, so load 221 may be a
     box waiting on a disk; PSI alone called that 7% box starved. The alert carries `io some`
     and `memory full` beside it so the reader can see which of the three it is.
  4. The swap rule asks nothing about CPU. A swapfile with nothing left is worth saying on an
     idle box: it is a statement about the next memory squeeze, not about now, since from there
     the kernel's reclaim has nowhere to go but the OOM killer. Time to exhaustion comes from
     this watch's own samples, the way disk-fill-watch computes time to full.
  5. Hold a crossing for the grace window before alerting, so a build burst does not fire it.
     Hold a CPU clear while the box is stalled (more tasks blocked than it has cores, or `io
     some` at or over --io-hold): `cpu some` falls when tasks wait on a disk instead of a CPU,
     and on 2026-11-02T10:25Z the old clear said "CLEARED" at load 851 with 723 tasks blocked.
  6. Name the consumers. CPU is attributed by a short delta of per-process CPU time, never by
     one `ps` reading, because the processes eating this box are thousands of short-lived
     children. Swap is attributed by `VmSwap` per process. Both are grouped by command and by
     the agent session or lane worker that owns them, so the alert says "115 workers, 181 GiB
     of swap in omp" rather than naming a pid nobody can act on.

It also counts the box's agents, because on these boxes the dial that causes this is the number
of concurrent workers, not any one of them.

It also watches two kernel stalls that make a box unable to stop or start agent boxes
(dispatch://INFRA-993, sami-devbox 2026-11-02 and 11-03): a WRITEBACK SWITCH STALL, where the
kernel moves a dead cgroup's inodes to its parent's writeback at the crawl of a 7.0 kernel
without torvalds f6988c90671e (`writeback:inode_switch_wbs` at or over SWITCH_RATE per second
for the grace window, counted by `sudo -n perf stat` only while a `+inode_switch_wbs` kworker is
busy or the crossing is open); and UMOUNT HUNG, an umount or fusermount3 in D state for over
UMOUNT_HUNG seconds, timed from /proc/<pid>/sched. Both say the reset plan and the cause.

When either stall alert opens, the run takes the evidence that says WHICH HALF of that kernel
bug this box has, once per episode and never twice in a minute: the kernel stack and wchan of
every task in D state, `echo w > /proc/sysrq-trigger` and the blocked-task traces it writes
(read back with a bounded `dmesg --since`), and the writeback switch kworkers with their CPU
time. It goes into the same report the alert names, under WHICH HALF OF THE STALL, with the
reading in plain words: `cleanup_offline_cgwb` in the stacks is the scanning half, which kernel
7.0.0-1017 fixes, and `cgroup_writeback_umount` without it is the waiting half, which 1017 does
not fix and which needs the box images split off the shared filesystem (capture_stacks;
kernel-stall-options.md). `--capture-stacks` takes it by hand and prints the section.

Alerts go over Envoy to agent sessions only, never to a person: the box-health lead's role,
falling back to the SRE session when nobody holds it. Each message is at most 600 characters and
names a report file under the state directory that holds the full reasons and the consumer
tables. A crossing still open is said again only on a new fact: a figure doubled or halved past
the worst this crossing has already said, or a command newly at the top of its consumers; never
because time passed.

It changes one thing about the box, and only this one: when the kernel is holding more than
DYING_THRESHOLD dead cgroups and io pressure is low, each run asks user.slice to reclaim a
bounded number of bytes so those groups can go, in steps, at most RECLAIM_BYTES and at most
once a minute, stopping the moment io pressure reaches the bar (reclaim_dying, and
load-swap-watch.md). It still kills nothing, stops no worker and changes no limit.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import fcntl
import json
import os
import re
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import time
import urllib.error
import urllib.request
from pathlib import Path

# Box health on this machine belongs to the SRE session: not to the chief of staff (Sami,
# 2026-10-31: "There's an SRE. They can handle it."), and no longer to the INFRA lead, who
# owned this detector and handed the lane over. Neither hears a box alert.
SRE = "01a18385-f8c8-74a9-b6ff-694fb5fe87a7"

# The box-health lead owns box resources (dispatch://INFRA-716) and holds this Envoy role, so a
# crossing goes to the role, not to a session id that moves when the lane changes hands.
BOX_HEALTH_ROLE_TOPIC = "notifications.role.lead-box-health"

DEFAULT_ENVOY_URL = "http://127.0.0.1:9020"
GIB = 1073741824

UUID_RE = re.compile(r"[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12}")
# An ossv lane worker's name, from the path its process stands in or its brief:
# /home/sami/work/cold/workers/c-libc-str.md, /home/sami/work/workers/c-libc-str/...
WORKER_RE = re.compile(r"/workers/([A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_.-]*?)(?:\.md|\.jsonl|/|\s|\Z)")
# The lane worker's STATE directory, which holds its live session id. `\S*` would swallow omp's
# `@` prompt-file prefix into the path, so the directory starts at a `/` and holds no `@`.
OSSV_WORKER_RE = re.compile(r"(?P<dir>/[^\s@]*?/\.ossv-workers/(?P<name>[^/\s]+))(?:/|\s|$)")

# Where the state file goes, as disk-fill-watch places its own: the big disk on an agent box,
# ~/.local/state on a machine without one.
_NVME_STATE = Path("/mnt/nvme/sami/tmp/load-swap-watch/state.json")
DEFAULT_STATE = (
    _NVME_STATE
    if os.access("/mnt/nvme/sami/tmp", os.W_OK)
    else Path(os.environ.get("XDG_STATE_HOME", str(Path.home() / ".local/state"))) / "load-swap-watch/state.json"
)

# The CPU bar, taken from the readings of 2026-10-29/30 rather than from a round number, and
# paired with a load over the core count (see verdict). Healthy boxes measured: ossv-agent-open
# at 01:32Z, cpu some avg60 7% at load 27 on 32 cores, answering normally; sami-leanperf idle,
# 0% at load 1 on 160. Starved boxes measured: ossv-agent-open during the six-lane incident,
# 80% at load 251 on 32; sami-rocqperf through the night, 42% to 65% at load 204-310 on 64, its
# own lanes visibly slow at the low end; ossv-agent-open2 at 01:32Z, 55% at load 76 on 32. The
# lowest starved reading is 42% and the highest healthy one is 7%, so the bar goes in that
# empty band, below every starved reading with margin and five times the highest healthy one.
CPU_STALL = 35.0

# A CPU crossing does not clear while `io some avg60` is at or over this, nor while more tasks are
# blocked than the box has cores. `cpu some` counts only tasks that are runnable, so a box whose
# tasks are stuck in uninterruptible sleep reads as having CPU to spare. The clears sami-rocqperf
# sent between 2026-11-01 and 2026-11-03: the one that held (11-01T12:45Z; load 8 at 14:20Z) had
# io some 6%; the four that did not were at 94% (11-02T10:25Z, 723 blocked), 91%
# (11-03T00:32Z, 329 blocked), 93% (00:36Z, 167 blocked) and 68% (01:39Z, 11 blocked; the crossing
# re-opened 8 minutes later and alerted again at 02:08Z). 50% sits between the two groups.
IO_HOLD = 50.0

# A writeback switch stall (dispatch://INFRA-993). When a cgroup that wrote millions of inodes
# dies, the kernel moves each of its inodes to the parent cgroup's writeback, and on these 7.0
# kernels (no torvalds f6988c90671e, which is in v7.3) `cleanup_offline_cgwb` re-walks every inode
# it already queued on each pass, so the move crawls at 1-200 inodes/s. Every umount and
# fusermount3 then waits in cgroup_writeback_umount for hours, agent boxes cannot stop or start,
# and sync() hangs. sami-devbox, 2026-11-03 12:45-13:03Z: `writeback:inode_switch_wbs` at
# 57-200/s for over an hour, all from one dead cgroup, `kworker/N:M+inode_switch_wbs` at 55-98% CPU
# for 1 h 40 min. The rate is read with `perf stat` (PERF) only while such a worker is busy or a
# crossing is open. The bar is a third of the lowest stall reading (57/s), so it still holds as
# the move slows; healthy bursts pass it too (sami-rocqperf: 24 of 81 ten-second counts), and
# what keeps them out is the busy worker the rule also asks of every run (writeback_finding).
# The measurements are in load-swap-watch.md.
SWITCH_RATE = 20.0
SWITCH_SAMPLE = 5.0
SWITCH_WORKER = "+inode_switch_wbs"
PERF = ("sudo", "-n", "perf")
SWITCH_EVENTS = ("writeback:inode_switch_wbs", "writeback:inode_switch_wbs_queue")
# A teardown umount or fusermount3 in D state longer than this is waiting on that stall: on
# sami-devbox on 2026-11-03 they were blocked 30 min to 3.4 h.
UMOUNT_HUNG = 300.0
HUNG_COMMANDS = ("umount", "fusermount3")
PROC = Path("/proc")
# What the box-health lead does about either: the reset runbook, not a reboot.
RESET_PLAN = "sami-rocqperf:/mnt/nvme/sami/scratch/devbox-provision/reset-plan-20261103.md"

# The one thing this watch does to the box, and the only one: a bounded reclaim of the dead
# cgroups the kernel is still holding (dispatch://INFRA-993).
#
# A cgroup whose processes are all gone stays alive while it still holds cached pages, and
# every ended agent box, session scope and job leaves one. On sami-devbox on 2026-11-04 at
# 19:01Z, after the 16:40-18:05Z stall and the reset, there were 928 of them, rising about 6 a
# minute. That count is what the writeback switch stall grows out of: the kernel moves each
# dead cgroup's inodes to its parent one at a time, and on these 7.0 kernels that crawls.
#
# Asking the parent slice to reclaim a bounded number of bytes frees those pages and the
# kernel then lets the empty groups go. Measured by hand on this box at 19:02-19:03Z: one 4G
# write took 476 ms and removed 88 groups; six 16G writes took 2.0, 1.3, 2.7, 2.8, 3.9 and
# 8.3 s and took the count from 840 to 135, with /proc/pressure/io full avg10 peaking at 0.98.
#
# So: never below DYING_THRESHOLD, never while io pressure is at or over RECLAIM_IO_HOLD,
# never more than RECLAIM_BYTES in one run, never more than once a RECLAIM_GAP, and in steps
# of RECLAIM_STEP so the io pressure can be read between them and the run stopped early. The
# write needs root and goes through `sudo -n`, which on this box needs no password and never
# waits for one, as `perf` above already does.
DYING_THRESHOLD = 400
RECLAIM_BYTES = 16 * GIB
RECLAIM_STEP = 4 * GIB
# Well under IO_HOLD (50), which is the bar for a box whose tasks are stuck on a disk. This is
# the bar for not making a busy disk busier: the hand runs above never passed 0.98.
RECLAIM_IO_HOLD = 5.0
RECLAIM_GAP = 60.0
# One run never spends longer than this starting steps, and one step never runs longer than
# RECLAIM_STEP_TIMEOUT. The unit's TimeoutStartSec is 120 s and an alerting pass already
# spends about 16 s of it.
RECLAIM_SECONDS = 20.0
RECLAIM_STEP_TIMEOUT = 25.0
RECLAIM_PATH = "/sys/fs/cgroup/user.slice/memory.reclaim"
CGROUP_STAT = "/sys/fs/cgroup/cgroup.stat"

# Per-host settings, by the host's short name. Every box runs this same script from its own
# ~/.dotfiles, so a box whose CPU is meant to be full says so here, in git, where a reviewer sees
# it, and not in a drop-in on that one machine that nobody reads from anywhere else.
HOST_SETTINGS: dict[str, dict] = {
    # sami-leanperf (160 vCPU) is the Lean timing-benchmark box (dispatch://INFRA-993,
    # leanperf-load.md): its bursts are benchmark windows that hold all 160 cores by design, p95
    # 92% CPU over 1,055 ten-minute samples, peak load 1,246. Its 7 CPU messages in the 48 h to
    # 2026-11-03 (cpu some avg60 76-83% at load 195 on 160) had no reaction. A raised bar would
    # still fire at every benchmark peak, where cpu some nears 100%, so the CPU rule is off there.
    # Swap and memory still alert: an OOM kill is not intended on any box.
    "sami-leanperf": {"cpu_rule": False},
}

# A crossing still open is said again only when it is clearly worse than when it was last said:
# load, or tasks blocked past the core count, at least doubled; swap free or MemAvailable at
# least halved (and by at least 1% of the total, so a figure already near zero cannot halve its
# way into a message every run).
WORSE = 2.0

# Every Envoy message stays under this, and the full detail goes to a report file it names.
MESSAGE_LIMIT = 600
REPORT_DAYS = 7

CLOCK_TICK = os.sysconf("SC_CLK_TCK")


def log(message: str) -> None:
    stamp = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())
    print(f"{stamp} {message}", flush=True)


def iso(epoch: float | None) -> str:
    if not epoch:
        return "unknown"
    return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime(epoch))


def human_time(seconds: float) -> str:
    seconds = max(0.0, float(seconds))
    if seconds < 90:
        return f"{seconds:.0f}s"
    if seconds < 5400:
        return f"{seconds / 60:.0f} min"
    if seconds < 172800:
        return f"{seconds / 3600:.1f} h"
    return f"{seconds / 86400:.1f} days"


def gib(value: float) -> str:
    return f"{value / GIB:.1f} GiB"


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Envoy


class Envoy:
    """The listener this machine's sessions use, resolved the way scripts/envoy resolves it."""

    def __init__(self, timeout: float = 8.0) -> None:
        path = os.environ.get("ENVOY_CONFIG_FILE") or str(
            Path(os.environ.get("XDG_CONFIG_HOME", str(Path.home() / ".config"))) / "legion/envoy.json"
        )
        try:
            config = json.loads(Path(path).read_text()).get("envoy") or {}
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            config = {}
        if config.get("enabled") is False:
            raise SystemExit(f"{path} sets envoy.enabled to false: Envoy is off")
        self.url = (os.environ.get("ENVOY_URL") or config.get("url") or DEFAULT_ENVOY_URL).rstrip("/")
        self.timeout = timeout
        token = os.environ.get("ENVOY_TOKEN") or config.get("token") or ""
        if token.startswith("!"):
            try:
                done = subprocess.run(
                    ["/bin/sh", "-c", token[1:]],
                    capture_output=True,
                    text=True,
                    timeout=10,
                    stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                )
                token = done.stdout.strip() if done.returncode == 0 else ""
            except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
                token = ""
        self.token = token

    def request(self, method: str, path: str, body: dict | None = None) -> tuple[int, dict | list]:
        data = json.dumps(body).encode() if body is not None else None
        request = urllib.request.Request(self.url + path, data=data, method=method)
        if data is not None:
            request.add_header("Content-Type", "application/json")
        if self.token:
            request.add_header("Authorization", f"Bearer {self.token}")
        try:
            with urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=self.timeout) as answer:
                raw = answer.read().decode() or "{}"
                return answer.status, json.loads(raw)
        except urllib.error.HTTPError as error:
            raw = error.read().decode() or "{}"
            try:
                return error.code, json.loads(raw)
            except ValueError:
                return error.code, {"error": raw.strip()}
        except (urllib.error.URLError, socket.timeout, ValueError, OSError) as error:
            return 0, {"error": str(error)}

    def send(self, session_id: str, message: str) -> bool:
        status, body = self.request(
            "POST",
            "/v1/messages/send",
            {"target_session": session_id, "message": message, "source": "envoy"},
        )
        if status != 200:
            log(f"  send to {session_id} failed: {status} {body}")
        return status == 200

    def publish(self, topic: str, message: str) -> bool:
        status, body = self.request(
            "POST",
            "/v1/messages/publish",
            {"topic": topic, "message": message, "source": "envoy"},
        )
        if status != 200:
            log(f"  publish to {topic} failed: {status} {body}")
        return status == 200


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the box


def read_text(path: str) -> str:
    try:
        return Path(path).read_text()
    except OSError:
        return ""


def pressure(resource: str) -> dict[str, float]:
    """`some` and `full` averages from /proc/pressure/<resource>, as percentages of wall time.

    `some avg60` is the number this watch decides on for CPU: the share of the last minute in
    which at least one runnable task was waiting for a CPU. A box with no PSI (a kernel without
    CONFIG_PSI) returns {}, and the CPU rule then falls back to the run queue per core.
    """
    found: dict[str, float] = {}
    for line in read_text(f"/proc/pressure/{resource}").splitlines():
        parts = line.split()
        if not parts:
            continue
        for item in parts[1:]:
            key, sep, value = item.partition("=")
            if sep and key.startswith("avg"):
                try:
                    found[f"{parts[0]}_{key}"] = float(value)
                except ValueError:
                    pass
    return found


def meminfo() -> dict[str, int]:
    found = {}
    for line in read_text("/proc/meminfo").splitlines():
        key, _, rest = line.partition(":")
        parts = rest.split()
        if parts:
            try:
                found[key] = int(parts[0]) * 1024
            except ValueError:
                pass
    return found


def vmstat() -> dict[str, int]:
    found = {}
    for line in read_text("/proc/vmstat").splitlines():
        parts = line.split()
        if len(parts) == 2:
            try:
                found[parts[0]] = int(parts[1])
            except ValueError:
                pass
    return found


def run_queue() -> tuple[int, int]:
    """(procs_running, procs_blocked) from /proc/stat: the runnable and the uninterruptible.

    Load average is the sum of both over time, so it cannot tell a box short of CPU from a box
    waiting on a disk. These two can, and the alert prints them.
    """
    running = blocked = 0
    for line in read_text("/proc/stat").splitlines():
        if line.startswith("procs_running "):
            running = int(line.split()[1])
        elif line.startswith("procs_blocked "):
            blocked = int(line.split()[1])
    return running, blocked


def _proc_ticks(base: Path) -> int | None:
    """utime + stime of the task under one /proc/<pid> directory, in clock ticks, or None."""
    try:
        data = (base / "stat").read_text()
        fields = data[data.rfind(")") + 2 :].split()
        return int(fields[11]) + int(fields[12])
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        return None


def _exec_start(base: Path) -> float | None:
    """`se.exec_start` from /proc/<pid>/sched: ms since boot when the task last ran on a CPU."""
    try:
        for line in (base / "sched").read_text().splitlines():
            if line.startswith("se.exec_start"):
                return float(line.split(":", 1)[1])
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        pass
    return None


def kernel_stalls(proc: Path | None = None) -> dict:
    """The writeback switch workers busy now, and every umount or fusermount3 in D state with how
    long it has been blocked, from one walk of /proc that reads each process's comm.

    All of it is world-readable, so no sudo: a root task's comm, stat, sched and cmdline can be
    read by the watch's own user. A kworker's comm ends in `+inode_switch_wbs` only while it is
    running a work item of that queue (`-inode_switch_wbs` once idle): on sami-devbox during the
    stall of 2026-11-03 one was busy in 60 of 60 one-second samples, on sami-rocqperf at the same
    hour in 7 of 60. The rate itself is not read here (switch_rate).

    Time blocked is not process age. A task in D state does not run, so `se.exec_start` in
    /proc/<pid>/sched, the last time it was on a CPU, is when it blocked, and /proc/uptime minus
    that is how long. devbox/sami-devbox-check judges its D1 line the same way.
    """
    proc = proc or PROC
    try:
        uptime = float(read_text(str(proc / "uptime")).split()[0])
    except (IndexError, ValueError):
        uptime = 0.0
    workers: list[dict] = []
    hung: list[dict] = []
    try:
        names = os.listdir(proc)
    except OSError:
        names = []
    for name in names:
        if not name.isdigit():
            continue
        # One raw read per process: half the CPU of Path.read_text over ~4,200 processes on
        # sami-rocqperf (0.075 s against 0.14 s), and only the matches read anything more.
        try:
            handle = os.open(f"{proc}/{name}/comm", os.O_RDONLY)
            try:
                comm = os.read(handle, 64).decode("utf8", "replace").strip()
            finally:
                os.close(handle)
        except OSError:
            continue
        base = proc / name
        if comm.endswith(SWITCH_WORKER):
            workers.append({"pid": int(name), "comm": comm, "ticks": _proc_ticks(base)})
            continue
        if comm not in HUNG_COMMANDS:
            continue
        try:
            data = (base / "stat").read_text()
            state = data[data.rfind(")") + 2 :].split()[0]
        except (OSError, IndexError):
            continue
        started = _exec_start(base)
        if state != "D" or started is None or not uptime:
            continue
        try:
            argv = (base / "cmdline").read_bytes().decode("utf8", "replace").split("\0")
        except OSError:
            argv = []
        # The mount point is the last argument that is not an option: `umount <dir>`,
        # `fusermount3 -u <dir>`.
        mount = next((arg for arg in reversed(argv[1:]) if arg and not arg.startswith("-")), "")
        hung.append({"pid": int(name), "comm": comm, "mount": mount, "blocked": max(0.0, uptime - started / 1000)})
    hung.sort(key=lambda task: -task["blocked"])
    return {"switch_workers": workers, "umounts": hung}


def switch_rate(seconds: float, workers: list[dict], proc: Path | None = None) -> dict:
    """`writeback:inode_switch_wbs` events per second over `seconds`, counted by `perf stat`.

    The tracepoint fires once per inode actually moved to another cgroup's writeback, which is
    the work that crawls in this stall. `perf stat` only counts (no ring buffer, no trace file),
    and it needs root, so it runs under `sudo -n`, which never waits for a password. Its cost is
    paid only while a switch worker is busy or a crossing is open (run_once): `sudo -n perf stat
    -a -- sleep 1` took 1.8 s of CPU on sami-rocqperf at load 300 on 64 cores. The CPU share of
    each busy switch worker over the same interval comes with it, for the report.

    A failure returns rate None and says why; it never reads as a rate of zero.
    """
    proc = proc or PROC
    command = [*PERF, "stat", "-x,", "-e", ",".join(SWITCH_EVENTS), "-a", "--", "sleep", f"{seconds:g}"]
    before = {worker["pid"]: _proc_ticks(proc / str(worker["pid"])) for worker in workers}
    start = time.monotonic()
    reading = {"rate": None, "switched": None, "queued": None, "seconds": seconds, "workers": [], "error": ""}
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(command, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=seconds + 60, cwd="/")
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as error:
        reading["error"] = f"`{' '.join(command)}` did not run: {error}"
        return reading
    elapsed = max(time.monotonic() - start, 1e-3)
    for worker in workers:
        then, now = before.get(worker["pid"]), _proc_ticks(proc / str(worker["pid"]))
        cpu = (now - then) / CLOCK_TICK / elapsed if None not in (then, now) else None
        reading["workers"].append(dict(worker, cpu=cpu))
    counts: dict[str, int] = {}
    for line in done.stderr.splitlines():
        fields = line.split(",")
        if len(fields) >= 3 and fields[2] in SWITCH_EVENTS:
            try:
                counts[fields[2]] = int(float(fields[0]))
            except ValueError:
                pass
    if done.returncode != 0 or SWITCH_EVENTS[0] not in counts:
        detail = (done.stderr.strip().splitlines() or [f"exit {done.returncode}"])[-1]
        reading["error"] = f"`{' '.join(command)}` gave no count (exit {done.returncode}): {detail[:200]}"
        return reading
    reading["switched"] = counts[SWITCH_EVENTS[0]]
    reading["queued"] = counts.get(SWITCH_EVENTS[1])
    reading["rate"] = reading["switched"] / seconds
    return reading


# ------------------------------------------------- which half of the stall this box has

# Both halves of this kernel bug look the same from outside: umounts wedged in D state, a
# kworker at 100%, container starts failing with "failed to pre-register with sysbox-fs".
# Only the kernel stacks of the blocked tasks tell them apart, and the answer decides whether a
# reboot is worth a maintenance window (kernel-stall-options.md §1b, dispatch://INFRA-993):
#
#   the SCANNING half -- `cleanup_offline_cgwb()` re-walks a dying cgroup's inode lists from the
#   same end on every pass, so draining one is quadratic (torvalds f6988c90671e). Ubuntu's
#   linux-gcp-7.0 7.0.0-1017 carries that fix and this machine can install it.
#
#   the WAITING half -- `cgroup_writeback_umount()` still does a global `synchronize_rcu()` and
#   `flush_workqueue(isw_wq)`, so every umount waits on every other superblock's switches. The
#   fix is 31c1d19ead2c, mainline 7.2 only, which no Ubuntu 24.04 kernel will ever carry, and
#   the answer becomes splitting the box images off the one shared filesystem.
#
# Every stall so far ended in a reset that took the evidence with it. So the moment a stall
# alert opens, this takes the evidence once -- the D-state tasks' stacks and wchans, `echo w >
# /proc/sysrq-trigger` and the blocked-task traces it writes, and the switch kworkers with
# their CPU time -- and writes it into the same report the alert already names, with the
# reading in plain words.
SCAN_FUNCTION = "cleanup_offline_cgwb"
WAIT_FUNCTION = "cgroup_writeback_umount"
# What makes a sysrq trace part of this stall, so a flood of unrelated blocked tasks is counted
# rather than kept.
STALL_FUNCTIONS = (SCAN_FUNCTION, WAIT_FUNCTION, "inode_switch_wbs", "isw_prepare_wbs_switch")
# What a switch kworker carries in its comm (`kworker/5:2+inode_switch_wbs`) or in its stack.
SWITCH_NAMES = ("inode_switch_wbs", SCAN_FUNCTION)
# Bounds, because a stall is when the box can least afford a big capture: one `echo w` on this
# box at 2026-11-04T21:14Z wrote 3,701 dmesg lines with nothing wrong, and a stalled box has
# hundreds of tasks in D state.
STACK_MAX_TASKS = 40
STACK_MAX_WORKERS = 20
STACK_MAX_DMESG = 4000
STACK_KEEP_BLOCKS = 25
STACK_HEAD_LINES = 30
STACK_SETTLE = 2.0
# sysrq w writes a lot of lines, so: once per episode (run_once), and never twice in a minute.
STACK_GAP = 60.0
SYSRQ_PATH = "/proc/sysrq-trigger"
STACK_HEADING = "WHICH HALF OF THE STALL (stack evidence)"


def d_state_tasks(proc: Path | None = None, limit: int = STACK_MAX_TASKS) -> list[dict]:
    """Every task in state D now, with its comm, its wchan and how long it has been blocked.

    The umount and fusermount3 come first, since their wait is the question, then the
    longest-blocked. comm, stat, wchan and sched are world-readable; the stack is not
    (read_kernel_stacks). Time blocked is read as kernel_stalls reads it: a task in D state does
    not run, so `se.exec_start` is when it blocked.
    """
    proc = proc or PROC
    try:
        uptime = float(read_text(str(proc / "uptime")).split()[0])
    except (IndexError, ValueError):
        uptime = 0.0
    tasks: list[dict] = []
    try:
        names = os.listdir(proc)
    except OSError:
        names = []
    for name in names:
        if not name.isdigit():
            continue
        base = proc / name
        try:
            data = (base / "stat").read_text()
            state = data[data.rfind(")") + 2 :].split()[0]
        except (OSError, IndexError):
            continue
        if state != "D":
            continue
        started = _exec_start(base)
        tasks.append(
            {
                "pid": int(name),
                "comm": data[data.find("(") + 1 : data.rfind(")")],
                "wchan": read_text(str(base / "wchan")).strip() or "unreadable",
                "blocked": max(0.0, uptime - started / 1000) if started is not None and uptime else None,
            }
        )
    tasks.sort(key=lambda task: (task["comm"] not in HUNG_COMMANDS, -(task["blocked"] or 0)))
    return tasks[:limit]


def read_kernel_stacks(pids: list[int], timeout: float = 30.0) -> tuple[dict[int, str], str]:
    """`/proc/<pid>/stack` for each pid, in one `sudo -n` run, and why it could not be read.

    This is the decisive reading: it names the kernel function the task is in, where wchan names
    only the outermost one. The file is root-only, and one shell loop rather than one sudo per
    pid, since a stalled box has dozens of blocked tasks and each sudo costs a fork.
    """
    if not pids:
        return {}, ""
    script = 'for pid in "$@"; do echo "##PID $pid"; cat "/proc/$pid/stack" 2>&1 || true; done'
    command = ["sudo", "-n", "sh", "-c", script, "stacks", *[str(pid) for pid in pids]]
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(command, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout, cwd="/")
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as error:
        return {}, f"`sudo -n cat /proc/<pid>/stack` did not run: {error}"
    if done.returncode != 0 and not done.stdout.strip():
        detail = (done.stderr.strip().splitlines() or [f"exit {done.returncode}"])[-1]
        return {}, f"`sudo -n cat /proc/<pid>/stack` failed (exit {done.returncode}): {detail[:200]}"
    stacks: dict[int, str] = {}
    pid: int | None = None
    for line in done.stdout.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("##PID "):
            try:
                pid = int(line.split()[1])
            except (IndexError, ValueError):
                pid = None
            else:
                stacks[pid] = ""
            continue
        if pid is not None and line.strip():
            stacks[pid] = f"{stacks[pid]}\n{line.strip()}".strip()
    return stacks, ""


def switch_kworkers(proc: Path | None = None, limit: int = STACK_MAX_WORKERS) -> tuple[list[dict], str]:
    """The kworkers doing writeback switching, by name or by stack, with their CPU time.

    A kworker names its work item in its comm while it runs one (`kworker/5:2+inode_switch_wbs`),
    so the comm finds the switch workers for free. One that is inside `cleanup_offline_cgwb`
    under another work item does not say so in its comm, so the stacks of the kworkers that are
    running or blocked are read too -- those only, since the idle pool here is hundreds of
    threads and reading them all would cost more than the capture is worth.
    """
    proc = proc or PROC
    candidates: list[dict] = []
    try:
        names = os.listdir(proc)
    except OSError:
        names = []
    for name in names:
        if not name.isdigit():
            continue
        base = proc / name
        try:
            data = (base / "stat").read_text()
            comm = data[data.find("(") + 1 : data.rfind(")")]
            state = data[data.rfind(")") + 2 :].split()[0]
        except (OSError, IndexError):
            continue
        if not comm.startswith("kworker/"):
            continue
        named = any(piece in comm for piece in SWITCH_NAMES)
        if not named and state not in ("R", "D"):
            continue
        ticks = _proc_ticks(base)
        candidates.append(
            {
                "pid": int(name),
                "comm": comm,
                "state": state,
                "named": named,
                # A worker running a work item of this queue says so with `+`; one that last ran
                # one and is now idle says so with `-`, and is kept because its CPU time is the
                # record of how long the drain has had it (kernel_stalls).
                "busy": comm.endswith(SWITCH_WORKER),
                "cpu_seconds": ticks / CLOCK_TICK if ticks is not None else None,
            }
        )
    candidates.sort(key=lambda worker: (not worker["busy"], not worker["named"], -(worker["cpu_seconds"] or 0)))
    candidates = candidates[: limit * 2]
    stacks, error = read_kernel_stacks([worker["pid"] for worker in candidates])
    workers = []
    for worker in candidates:
        stack = stacks.get(worker["pid"], "")
        if worker["named"] or any(piece in stack for piece in SWITCH_NAMES):
            workers.append(dict(worker, stack=stack))
    return workers[:limit], error


def dmesg_since(start: float, timeout: float = 120.0) -> tuple[list[str], str]:
    """The kernel log since `start`, bounded by the kernel's own filter and then by line count.

    `dmesg --since` rather than the whole buffer: the buffer on this box holds hours of other
    machinery, and what is wanted is only what the sysrq this run issued produced.
    """
    command = ["sudo", "-n", "dmesg", "--time-format=iso", "--since", f"@{int(start)}"]
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(command, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout, cwd="/")
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as error:
        return [], f"`{' '.join(command)}` did not run: {error}"
    if done.returncode != 0:
        detail = (done.stderr.strip().splitlines() or [f"exit {done.returncode}"])[-1]
        return [], f"`{' '.join(command)}` failed (exit {done.returncode}): {detail[:200]}"
    return done.stdout.splitlines(), ""


def dmesg_blocks(lines: list[str]) -> list[list[str]]:
    """The kernel log cut into one block per blocked task, as `echo w` prints them: a `task:`
    line and the call trace under it. Anything before the first `task:` line is its own block."""
    blocks: list[list[str]] = []
    for line in lines:
        if " task:" in line or line.startswith("task:") or not blocks:
            blocks.append([])
        blocks[-1].append(line)
    return [block for block in blocks if block]


def sysrq_blocked_state(settle: float = STACK_SETTLE) -> dict:
    """Ask the kernel for every blocked task's stack, and read back only what it wrote.

    `echo w > /proc/sysrq-trigger` is the one thing here that touches the machine, and it only
    prints: it syncs nothing, unmounts nothing and writes to no filesystem. What it costs is
    kernel log lines, which is why the capture happens at most once an episode and never twice
    in a minute.
    """
    reading: dict = {"ok": False, "error": "", "lines": 0, "blocks": 0, "stall_blocks": 0, "kept": [], "text": ""}
    # A second of margin: the kernel's log clock and ours are not the same clock.
    start = time.time() - 1
    command = ["sudo", "-n", "sh", "-c", f"echo w > {SYSRQ_PATH}"]
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(command, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30, cwd="/")
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as error:
        reading["error"] = f"`{' '.join(command)}` did not run: {error}"
        return reading
    if done.returncode != 0:
        detail = (done.stderr.strip().splitlines() or [f"exit {done.returncode}"])[-1]
        reading["error"] = f"`{' '.join(command)}` failed (exit {done.returncode}): {detail[:200]}"
        return reading
    reading["ok"] = True
    time.sleep(settle)
    lines, error = dmesg_since(start)
    if error:
        reading["error"] = error
        return reading
    reading["lines"] = len(lines)
    blocks = dmesg_blocks(lines[:STACK_MAX_DMESG])
    reading["blocks"] = len(blocks)
    stall = [block for block in blocks if any(name in "\n".join(block) for name in STALL_FUNCTIONS)]
    reading["stall_blocks"] = len(stall)
    # The traces of this stall whole, and otherwise the head of what the sysrq printed, so a
    # capture that names neither function still shows what the kernel did say.
    kept = stall[:STACK_KEEP_BLOCKS] or [lines[:STACK_HEAD_LINES]]
    reading["kept"] = kept
    reading["text"] = "\n".join("\n".join(block) for block in kept)
    return reading


def half_verdict(texts: list[str]) -> str:
    """Which half of the bug the stacks name: scanning, waiting, neither, or unreadable.

    `cleanup_offline_cgwb` decides when it appears at all. `cgroup_writeback_umount` is in every
    umount's stack by construction -- the umount calls it -- so it only answers the question when
    the scan function appears nowhere and the time is therefore going into the wait.
    """
    joined = "\n".join(text for text in texts if text)
    if not joined.strip():
        return "unreadable"
    if SCAN_FUNCTION in joined:
        return "scanning"
    if WAIT_FUNCTION in joined:
        return "waiting"
    return "neither"


HALF_READING = {
    "scanning": (
        f"The stacks name {SCAN_FUNCTION}: the scanning half; kernel 7.0.0-1017 fixes this."
        " Take the maintenance window and install linux-gcp-7.0 7.0.0-1017.19~24.04.1."
    ),
    "waiting": (
        f"The stacks name {WAIT_FUNCTION}, or a wait inside it, and never {SCAN_FUNCTION}: the"
        " waiting half; 1017 does not fix this, the box images must be split off the shared"
        " filesystem."
    ),
    "neither": (
        f"The stacks name neither {SCAN_FUNCTION} nor {WAIT_FUNCTION}, so this capture does not"
        " say which half of the bug we have, and the stall may not be this bug at all."
    ),
    "unreadable": (
        "No kernel stack could be read, so this capture does not say which half of the bug we"
        " have. The reasons are above; `sudo -n` is what it needs."
    ),
}
HALF_SHORT = {
    "scanning": "the scanning half; 7.0.0-1017 fixes it",
    "waiting": "the waiting half; 1017 does not fix it",
    "neither": "neither function named",
    "unreadable": "no stack could be read",
}


def capture_stacks(when: float, proc: Path | None = None) -> dict:
    """The whole capture: D-state tasks with their stacks and wchans, the sysrq blocked-task
    traces, the switch kworkers with their CPU time, and the reading those add up to."""
    started = time.monotonic()
    tasks = d_state_tasks(proc)
    stacks, task_error = read_kernel_stacks([task["pid"] for task in tasks])
    for task in tasks:
        task["stack"] = stacks.get(task["pid"], "")
    workers, worker_error = switch_kworkers(proc)
    sysrq = sysrq_blocked_state()
    texts = [task["stack"] for task in tasks] + [worker["stack"] for worker in workers] + [sysrq["text"]]
    return {
        "at": when,
        "seconds": time.monotonic() - started,
        "tasks": tasks,
        "workers": workers,
        "sysrq": sysrq,
        "errors": [error for error in (task_error, worker_error) if error],
        "half": half_verdict(texts),
    }


def stack_section(capture: dict) -> str:
    """The capture as the report reads it, under one heading."""
    sysrq = capture["sysrq"]
    lines = [
        STACK_HEADING,
        f"Taken at {iso(capture['at'])} in {capture['seconds']:.1f} s, once for this episode"
        f" (never twice within {human_time(STACK_GAP)}).",
        "",
        f"The reading: {HALF_READING[capture['half']]}",
    ]
    for error in capture["errors"]:
        lines.append(f"Not read: {error}")
    lines += ["", f"Tasks in D state ({len(capture['tasks'])}, umount and fusermount3 first, at most {STACK_MAX_TASKS}):"]
    if not capture["tasks"]:
        lines.append("  none")
    for task in capture["tasks"]:
        blocked = human_time(task["blocked"]) if task["blocked"] is not None else "unknown"
        lines.append(f"  pid {task['pid']} {task['comm']}, blocked {blocked}, wchan {task['wchan']}")
        for frame in (task["stack"] or "stack unreadable").splitlines():
            lines.append(f"      {frame}")
    lines += ["", f"Writeback switch kworkers ({len(capture['workers'])}):"]
    if not capture["workers"]:
        lines.append("  none")
    for worker in capture["workers"]:
        cpu = f"{worker['cpu_seconds']:.1f} s of CPU" if worker["cpu_seconds"] is not None else "CPU time unreadable"
        doing = "running a switch work item now" if worker["busy"] else f"idle, state {worker['state']}"
        lines.append(f"  pid {worker['pid']} {worker['comm']} ({doing}), {cpu} since boot")
        for frame in (worker["stack"] or "stack unreadable").splitlines():
            lines.append(f"      {frame}")
    lines += ["", f"echo w > {SYSRQ_PATH} (blocked-task traces):"]
    if not sysrq["ok"] or sysrq["error"]:
        lines.append(f"  {sysrq['error'] or 'it did not run'}")
    if sysrq["ok"]:
        lines.append(
            f"  it wrote {sysrq['lines']} kernel log lines, {sysrq['blocks']} blocked-task traces,"
            f" of which {sysrq['stall_blocks']} name this stall; kept"
            f" {len(sysrq['kept'])} (at most {STACK_KEEP_BLOCKS})."
        )
        for line in sysrq["text"].splitlines():
            lines.append(f"  {line}")
    return "\n".join(lines)


def sample_box() -> dict:
    memory = meminfo()
    swap_total = memory.get("SwapTotal", 0)
    swap_free = memory.get("SwapFree", 0)
    running, blocked = run_queue()
    load = read_text("/proc/loadavg").split()
    cores = os.cpu_count() or 1
    return {
        "at": time.time(),
        "cores": cores,
        "cpu": pressure("cpu"),
        "io": pressure("io"),
        "memory_pressure": pressure("memory"),
        "load1": float(load[0]) if load else 0.0,
        "load5": float(load[1]) if len(load) > 1 else 0.0,
        "load15": float(load[2]) if len(load) > 2 else 0.0,
        "procs_running": running,
        "procs_blocked": blocked,
        "swap_total": swap_total,
        "swap_free": swap_free,
        "swap_used_fraction": (1 - swap_free / swap_total) if swap_total else 0.0,
        "mem_available": memory.get("MemAvailable", 0),
        "mem_total": memory.get("MemTotal", 0),
        "pswpin": vmstat().get("pswpin", 0),
        "dying": dying_descendants(),
        **kernel_stalls(),
    }


def dying_descendants() -> int | None:
    """How many dead cgroups the kernel still holds, from /sys/fs/cgroup/cgroup.stat.

    One world-readable file, no sudo. None when the line is not there (cgroup v1, or a kernel
    without it), and the reclaim below then never runs.
    """
    for line in read_text(CGROUP_STAT).splitlines():
        if line.startswith("nr_dying_descendants"):
            try:
                return int(line.split()[1])
            except (IndexError, ValueError):
                return None
    return None


def reclaim_dying(now: dict, options: argparse.Namespace, state_data: dict) -> dict | None:
    """Ask user.slice to reclaim a bounded number of bytes, so the kernel can free the dead
    cgroups holding those pages. The reading, or None when this run did not reclaim.

    Every refusal is a reason, and every run that reclaims is one log line with the count
    before, the count after, the bytes asked for, the seconds taken and the io pressure.
    """
    if not options.reclaim:
        return None
    before = now.get("dying")
    if before is None:
        return None
    if before < options.dying_threshold:
        return None
    io_before = (now.get("io") or {}).get("full_avg10")
    if io_before is None:
        log(f"{before} dying cgroups, but this kernel reports no io pressure; not reclaiming")
        return None
    if io_before >= options.reclaim_io_hold:
        log(
            f"{before} dying cgroups, but io full avg10 is {io_before:.2f}, at or over the"
            f" {options.reclaim_io_hold:g} this reclaim allows; not reclaiming"
        )
        return None
    last = float(state_data.get("last_reclaim") or 0)
    since = time.time() - last
    if since < RECLAIM_GAP:
        log(f"{before} dying cgroups; last reclaim {since:.0f}s ago, under the {RECLAIM_GAP:.0f}s gap")
        return None
    if options.dry_run:
        log(f"{before} dying cgroups, io full avg10 {io_before:.2f}; would reclaim up to {gib(options.reclaim_bytes)}")
        return None
    # Recorded before the first write, so a run killed mid-reclaim still holds the next one off
    # for a whole gap rather than letting the next pass start another.
    state_data["last_reclaim"] = time.time()
    asked = 0
    steps = 0
    stopped = ""
    io_after = io_before
    started = time.monotonic()
    while asked < options.reclaim_bytes:
        if time.monotonic() - started >= RECLAIM_SECONDS:
            stopped = f"this run's {RECLAIM_SECONDS:.0f}s was spent"
            break
        step = min(RECLAIM_STEP, options.reclaim_bytes - asked)
        command = ["sudo", "-n", "sh", "-c", f"echo {step} > {RECLAIM_PATH}"]
        try:
            done = subprocess.run(
                command,
                capture_output=True,
                text=True,
                timeout=RECLAIM_STEP_TIMEOUT,
                stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                cwd="/",
            )
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as error:
            stopped = f"`{' '.join(command)}` did not finish: {error}"
            break
        steps += 1
        if done.returncode != 0:
            # EAGAIN is the kernel saying it could not free that much, not a failure of ours.
            detail = (done.stderr.strip().splitlines() or [f"exit {done.returncode}"])[-1]
            stopped = f"the write was refused (exit {done.returncode}): {detail[:160]}"
            break
        asked += step
        io_after = pressure("io").get("full_avg10", io_after)
        if io_after >= options.reclaim_io_hold:
            stopped = f"io full avg10 reached {io_after:.2f}"
            break
    took = time.monotonic() - started
    after = dying_descendants()
    reading = {
        "at": time.time(),
        "before": before,
        "after": after,
        "asked": asked,
        "steps": steps,
        "seconds": took,
        "io_before": io_before,
        "io_after": io_after,
        "stopped": stopped,
    }
    # One line a person can read without any other file: what it found, what it asked for,
    # what it cost, and what the box's io pressure did while it ran.
    log(
        f"dying cgroup reclaim: {before} ->"
        f" {after if after is not None else '?'} dying cgroups"
        + (f" ({after - before:+d})" if after is not None else "")
        + f", asked {gib(asked)} in {steps} step(s), {took:.1f}s,"
        + f" io full avg10 {io_before:.2f} -> {io_after:.2f}"
        + (f"; stopped early: {stopped}" if stopped else "")
    )
    state_data["last_reclaim_reading"] = reading
    return reading


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the consumers


def cmdline_of(pid: int) -> str:
    try:
        return Path(f"/proc/{pid}/cmdline").read_bytes().decode("utf8", "replace").replace("\0", " ").strip()
    except OSError:
        return ""


def comm_of(pid: int) -> str:
    try:
        return Path(f"/proc/{pid}/comm").read_text().strip()
    except OSError:
        return ""


def ppid_of(pid: int) -> int:
    try:
        data = Path(f"/proc/{pid}/stat").read_text()
        return int(data[data.rfind(")") + 2 :].split()[1])
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        return 0


def age_of(pid: int) -> float:
    try:
        data = Path(f"/proc/{pid}/stat").read_text()
        ticks = int(data[data.rfind(")") + 2 :].split()[19])
        uptime = float(read_text("/proc/uptime").split()[0])
        return max(0.0, uptime - ticks / CLOCK_TICK)
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        return 0.0


def cpu_ticks_of(pid: int) -> int | None:
    """utime + stime of a process, in clock ticks, or None when it is gone."""
    try:
        data = Path(f"/proc/{pid}/stat").read_text()
        fields = data[data.rfind(")") + 2 :].split()
        return int(fields[11]) + int(fields[12])
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        return None


def _cwd_of(pid: int) -> str:
    """The directory a process stands in, or "". The disk-writer watch names this for the same
    reason: on a lane box the working directory says which lane's tree the work is in, which
    survives every inherited environment variable."""
    try:
        return os.readlink(f"/proc/{pid}/cwd")
    except OSError:
        return ""


# pane pid -> "<tmux session>:<window>.<pane>", read once per run. A lane box runs one tmux
# window per worker, so the pane is the address a person uses to look at the work.
PANES: dict[int, str] = {}
PANES_READ = False


def refresh_panes() -> None:
    global PANES_READ
    PANES_READ = True
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(
            ["tmux", "list-panes", "-a", "-F", "#{pane_pid} #{session_name}:#{window_name}.#{pane_index}"],
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            timeout=10,
            stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return
    if done.returncode != 0:
        return
    for line in done.stdout.splitlines():
        pane_pid, _, label = line.partition(" ")
        if pane_pid.isdigit() and label:
            PANES[int(pane_pid)] = label


def pane_of(pid: int, depth: int = 12) -> str:
    """The tmux pane a process runs under, found by walking up to a known pane pid."""
    here = pid
    for _ in range(depth):
        if here <= 1:
            break
        if here in PANES:
            return PANES[here]
        here = ppid_of(here)
    return ""


def environ_of(pid: int) -> dict[str, str]:
    found = {}
    try:
        raw = Path(f"/proc/{pid}/environ").read_bytes().decode("utf8", "replace")
    except OSError:
        return found
    for item in raw.split("\0"):
        key, sep, value = item.partition("=")
        if sep and key in ("OMP_SESSION_ID", "CLAUDE_SESSION_ID"):
            found[key] = value
    return found


def session_from_fds(pid: int, limit: int = 128) -> str:
    """The session id of the transcript this process holds open, or "".

    An `omp` started as `omp --resume <path>` carries no session id in its environment, and its
    command line is truncated in `ps`; the transcript it holds open names the session in full.
    """
    try:
        descriptors = os.listdir(f"/proc/{pid}/fd")
    except OSError:
        return ""
    for descriptor in descriptors[:limit]:
        try:
            target = os.readlink(f"/proc/{pid}/fd/{descriptor}")
        except OSError:
            continue
        if "/.omp/agent/sessions/" in target and target.endswith(".jsonl"):
            found = UUID_RE.findall(os.path.basename(target))
            if found:
                return found[0]
    return ""


# The sessions Envoy has live on THIS box, filled at most once per run and only when an alert
# is about to name processes. Empty with LIVE_READ False means "not looked up", which must read
# as "cannot verify" and never as "nothing is live": an Envoy that is down would otherwise make
# every process on the box look misattributed.
LIVE_SESSIONS: dict[str, dict] = {}
LIVE_READ = False

# How a session id reached this process. The environment is the weakest: it is INHERITED, so a
# session that started the box's supervisor over ssh stamps its own id on every descendant for
# the life of the box. On 2026-10-30 at 20:17:23Z that made this watch name 39 python3
# processes on ossv-agent-repo as 01a14b36 (VERIFY, which runs on sami-rocqperf) when the live
# owner was the repo lane 01a14b84.
SOURCE_PHRASE = {
    "worker": "the lane worker's own session file",
    "cmdline": "the session file it was resumed from",
    "fd": "the transcript it holds open",
    "environ": "the process environment (started it)",
}


def this_box(host: str) -> bool:
    """Whether an Envoy session row's host is this machine.

    A lane VM registers through the hub under its FQDN while `socket.gethostname()` can be
    either form, so the first label decides.
    """
    if not host:
        return False
    mine = socket.gethostname()
    return host == mine or host.split(".")[0] == mine.split(".")[0]


def refresh_live_sessions(envoy: "Envoy") -> None:
    """Ask Envoy which sessions are live on this box. One GET, at most once per run."""
    global LIVE_READ
    status, body = envoy.request("GET", "/v1/sessions")
    if status != 200 or not isinstance(body, list):
        log(f"  live sessions unavailable: GET /v1/sessions answered {status}; attribution unverified")
        return
    LIVE_SESSIONS.clear()
    for row in body:
        if isinstance(row, dict) and row.get("session_id") and this_box(str(row.get("host") or "")):
            LIVE_SESSIONS[str(row["session_id"])] = row
    LIVE_READ = True
    log(f"  {len(LIVE_SESSIONS)} session(s) live on this box, of {len(body)} Envoy knows")


def worker_dir_of(pid: int, depth: int = 8) -> str:
    """The `.ossv-workers/<name>` directory a process belongs to, from its ancestry, or "".

    `ossv-worker` starts each worker with `@<root>/.ossv-workers/<name>/message-*.md` and keeps
    the worker's live session id in `<that dir>/session`, which is the only lane-local record
    that follows a resume.
    """
    here = pid
    for _ in range(depth):
        if here <= 1:
            break
        for text in (cmdline_of(here), _cwd_of(here) + "/"):
            found = OSSV_WORKER_RE.search(text)
            if found:
                return found.group("dir")
        here = ppid_of(here)
    return ""


def worker_dir_session(directory: str) -> str:
    """The session id `ossv-worker` recorded for that worker, unless it is still unconfirmed."""
    if not directory:
        return ""
    try:
        if (Path(directory) / "session_unconfirmed").exists():
            return ""
        found = UUID_RE.findall((Path(directory) / "session").read_text())
    except OSError:
        return ""
    return found[0] if found else ""


def session_claim(pid: int, depth: int = 12) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """(the session id this process claims, where it came from), strongest source first.

    The environment comes LAST and never first. It is inherited across ssh, tmux and every
    exec, so it names whoever started the ancestor, which on a lane box is a session on another
    machine entirely. A process's own `omp` -- its resume path or the transcript it holds open
    -- and the lane worker's session file are about the process, not about its history.
    """
    worker_session = worker_dir_session(worker_dir_of(pid))
    if worker_session:
        return worker_session, "worker"
    here = pid
    for _ in range(depth):
        if here <= 1:
            break
        if comm_of(here) in ("omp", "claude", "node", "bun"):
            found = UUID_RE.findall(cmdline_of(here))
            if found:
                return found[0], "cmdline"
            found_fd = session_from_fds(here)
            if found_fd:
                return found_fd, "fd"
        here = ppid_of(here)
    here = pid
    for _ in range(depth):
        if here <= 1:
            break
        environment = environ_of(here)
        for key in ("OMP_SESSION_ID", "CLAUDE_SESSION_ID"):
            if environment.get(key):
                return environment[key], "environ"
        here = ppid_of(here)
    return "", ""


def session_of(pid: int, depth: int = 12) -> str:
    """The agent session a process belongs to, read from the process and never guessed."""
    return session_claim(pid, depth)[0]


def live_owner(pid: int) -> str:
    """The live session on this box that actually owns this process, or "".

    Its lane worker's session file first. Failing that, a box running exactly one live session
    has only one possible owner, which is the shape of ossv-agent-repo and ossv-agent-git; a box
    running several lanes has no such answer and this says so rather than picking one.
    """
    from_worker = worker_dir_session(worker_dir_of(pid))
    if from_worker and from_worker in LIVE_SESSIONS:
        return from_worker
    if LIVE_READ and len(LIVE_SESSIONS) == 1:
        return next(iter(LIVE_SESSIONS))
    return ""


def session_phrase(pid: int) -> str:
    """How one process's session is named in an alert, verified against what is live here."""
    claim, source = session_claim(pid)
    if claim and (not LIVE_READ or claim in LIVE_SESSIONS):
        return f"session {claim}"
    owner = live_owner(pid)
    if claim:
        text = f"session {claim} from {SOURCE_PHRASE.get(source, source)} (not live on this box)"
    else:
        text = "session unknown (no process in its ancestry names one)"
    if owner and owner != claim:
        text += f"; the live owner on this box is {owner}"
    elif not owner:
        text += "; no live session on this box could be matched to it"
    return text


def worker_of(pid: int, depth: int = 6) -> str:
    """The lane worker a process belongs to: its brief's name, its working directory, or "".

    A lane box runs one `omp` per worker under `w-<name>` of its tmux server, and the worker's
    name is in the brief path on its command line and in the directory it stands in. Both are
    read from the process; a box that is not a lane box simply has no match.
    """
    here = pid
    for _ in range(depth):
        if here <= 1:
            break
        found = WORKER_RE.search(cmdline_of(here))
        if found:
            return found.group(1)
        try:
            found = WORKER_RE.search(os.readlink(f"/proc/{here}/cwd") + "/")
            if found:
                return found.group(1)
        except OSError:
            pass
        here = ppid_of(here)
    return ""


def memory_of(pid: int) -> tuple[int, int]:
    """(VmRSS, VmSwap) of a process in bytes, from one read of /proc/<pid>/status (world-readable,
    so no sudo). A kernel thread has neither line and reads (0, 0)."""
    rss = swap = 0
    try:
        for line in Path(f"/proc/{pid}/status").read_text().splitlines():
            if line.startswith("VmRSS:"):
                rss = int(line.split()[1]) * 1024
            elif line.startswith("VmSwap:"):
                swap = int(line.split()[1]) * 1024
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        pass
    return rss, swap


def swap_of(pid: int) -> int:
    """VmSwap of a process, in bytes."""
    return memory_of(pid)[1]


def rss_of(pid: int) -> int:
    return memory_of(pid)[0]


def pids() -> list[int]:
    return [int(name) for name in os.listdir("/proc") if name.isdigit()]


def sample_cpu(seconds: float) -> tuple[list[dict], float]:
    """Per-process CPU over one short interval: (rows sorted by cores used, actual interval).

    A single `ps` column is useless here. The processes that filled this box's run queue were
    thousands of short-lived git, python and gzip children: every pid in a `ps` snapshot taken
    at 23:52Z was already gone at 23:53Z. A delta over an interval counts the work they did,
    and attributes what is left to the long-lived `omp` processes correctly.
    """
    before = {}
    for pid in pids():
        ticks = cpu_ticks_of(pid)
        if ticks is not None:
            before[pid] = ticks
    started = time.time()
    time.sleep(seconds)
    span = max(time.time() - started, 0.001)
    rows = []
    for pid, was in before.items():
        now = cpu_ticks_of(pid)
        if now is None or now <= was:
            continue
        rows.append({"pid": pid, "cores": (now - was) / CLOCK_TICK / span, "comm": comm_of(pid)})
    rows.sort(key=lambda row: -row["cores"])
    return rows, span


def sample_memory() -> list[dict]:
    """RSS and swap of every process holding either, sorted by swap held."""
    rows = []
    for pid in pids():
        rss, swap = memory_of(pid)
        if rss or swap:
            rows.append({"pid": pid, "rss": rss, "swap": swap, "comm": comm_of(pid)})
    rows.sort(key=lambda row: -row["swap"])
    return rows


def by_command(rows: list[dict], field: str) -> list[tuple[str, float, int]]:
    totals: dict[str, list[float]] = {}
    for row in rows:
        entry = totals.setdefault(row["comm"] or "?", [0.0, 0])
        entry[0] += row[field]
        entry[1] += 1
    ranked = [(name, total, int(count)) for name, (total, count) in totals.items()]
    ranked.sort(key=lambda item: -item[1])
    return ranked


def describe(pid: int) -> str:
    """One process, named the way a reader can act on it.

    Lane, verified session, working directory, tmux pane and age. The directory and the pane
    are here because they are properties of the process itself: an inherited environment
    variable can name the wrong session, but the tree a process stands in and the pane it runs
    under cannot be inherited from a session on another machine.
    """
    parts = []
    worker = worker_of(pid)
    if worker:
        parts.append(f"lane {worker}")
    parts.append(session_phrase(pid))
    cwd = _cwd_of(pid)
    if cwd:
        parts.append(f"cwd {cwd}")
    pane = pane_of(pid)
    if pane:
        parts.append(f"pane {pane}")
    elif PANES_READ:
        parts.append("no tmux pane")
    command = cmdline_of(pid) or comm_of(pid)
    if command:
        parts.append(command[:70])
    age = age_of(pid)
    if age:
        parts.append(f"up {human_time(age)}")
    return ", ".join(parts)


def agent_census() -> dict:
    """How many agents this box is carrying, and what they hold. The dial, on a lane box."""
    count = 0
    rss = swap = 0
    for pid in pids():
        if comm_of(pid) != "omp":
            continue
        count += 1
        held, swapped = memory_of(pid)
        rss += held
        swap += swapped
    return {"omp": count, "rss": rss, "swap": swap}


def measure_consumers(options: argparse.Namespace) -> dict:
    """Who used the CPU over a short interval, and who holds swap and memory, ranked by command.

    The CPU sample sleeps --sample-seconds between two walks of /proc, which makes this the one
    expensive step of a run (16 s measured on sami-rocqperf at load 321 with 2,900 processes). It
    runs only when a message is about to go, or when an open crossing is due its check for a new
    top consumer, and at most once per run however many conditions need it.
    """
    cpu_rows, span = sample_cpu(options.sample_seconds)
    mem_rows = sample_memory()
    return {
        "span": span,
        "cpu_rows": cpu_rows,
        "mem_rows": mem_rows,
        "ranked": {
            "cpu": by_command(cpu_rows, "cores"),
            "swap": by_command([row for row in mem_rows if row["swap"]], "swap"),
            "memory": by_command(mem_rows, "rss"),
        },
    }


def consumers_text(
    options: argparse.Namespace, envoy: "Envoy | None" = None, measured: dict | None = None
) -> str:
    """The consumer tables of a report: who uses the CPU now, and who holds swap and memory."""
    # Both lookups happen here and not every period: this function runs only when a report is
    # about to name processes. One `tmux list-panes`, one Envoy GET.
    refresh_panes()
    if envoy is not None:
        refresh_live_sessions(envoy)
    measured = measured or measure_consumers(options)
    cores = os.cpu_count() or 1
    census = agent_census()
    ranked = measured["ranked"]

    lines = [
        f"The box is carrying {census['omp']} omp processes holding {gib(census['rss'])} of RSS"
        f" and {gib(census['swap'])} of swap."
    ]
    if ranked["cpu"]:
        share = ", ".join(
            f"{name} {total:.1f} cores ({100 * total / cores:.0f}% of the box, {count} processes)"
            for name, total, count in ranked["cpu"][:5]
        )
        lines.append(f"CPU over a {measured['span']:.0f}s interval, by command: {share}.")
    if measured["cpu_rows"]:
        lines.append(f"Top CPU processes over that interval (of {cores} cores):")
        for row in measured["cpu_rows"][: options.top]:
            lines.append(f"  pid {row['pid']} {row['comm']} {row['cores']:.2f} cores -- {describe(row['pid'])}")
    if ranked["swap"]:
        share = ", ".join(f"{name} {gib(total)} ({count} processes)" for name, total, count in ranked["swap"][:5])
        lines.append(f"Swap held, by command: {share}.")
        lines.append("Top swap holders:")
        for row in [row for row in measured["mem_rows"] if row["swap"]][: options.top]:
            lines.append(f"  pid {row['pid']} {row['comm']} {gib(row['swap'])} -- {describe(row['pid'])}")
    if ranked["memory"]:
        share = ", ".join(f"{name} {gib(total)} ({count} processes)" for name, total, count in ranked["memory"][:5])
        lines.append(f"RSS held, by command (a page shared by two processes counts twice): {share}.")
        lines.append("Top RSS holders:")
        for row in sorted(measured["mem_rows"], key=lambda row: -row["rss"])[: options.top]:
            lines.append(f"  pid {row['pid']} {row['comm']} {gib(row['rss'])} -- {describe(row['pid'])}")
    return "\n".join(lines)


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- state


class State:
    """The one file this watch owns: its samples and its open crossings.

    Opened under an exclusive, non-blocking flock for the whole run, so a hand run and the timer
    can never both decide that a crossing is new; the loser skips its period.
    """

    def __init__(self, path: Path) -> None:
        self.path = path
        path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        self.handle = open(path, "a+")
        try:
            fcntl.flock(self.handle, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
            self.locked = True
        except OSError:
            self.locked = False
            self.data = {}
            return
        self.handle.seek(0)
        try:
            self.data = json.loads(self.handle.read() or "{}")
        except ValueError:
            self.data = {}

    def commit(self, options: argparse.Namespace) -> None:
        if options.dry_run and not options.write_state:
            return
        self.handle.seek(0)
        self.handle.truncate()
        json.dump(self.data, self.handle, indent=1, sort_keys=True)
        self.handle.flush()
        os.fsync(self.handle.fileno())

    def close(self) -> None:
        try:
            self.handle.close()
        except OSError:
            pass


def swap_drain_rate(samples: list[list[float]], window: float) -> tuple[float | None, float]:
    """(bytes per second of swap free-space fall, actual measurement window)."""
    if len(samples) < 2:
        return None, 0.0
    now = samples[-1][0]
    anchor = samples[0]
    for sample in samples:
        if now - sample[0] <= window:
            anchor = sample
            break
    span = now - anchor[0]
    if span <= 0:
        return None, 0.0
    return (anchor[1] - samples[-1][1]) / span, span


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the decision


# Each condition is judged apart and gets its own line. On 2026-10-30T01:32Z an escalation from
# ossv-agent-open read "STILL OUT OF CPU+SWAP" while that box carried load 27 on 32 cores with
# cpu some avg60 at 7%: a working box. The swap half was true and the CPU half had recovered
# half an hour before, because the headline named the union of everything the crossing had ever
# been rather than what was true at the time it spoke. A headline that overstates one half
# makes the next true alert cheaper to dismiss, which on these boxes is expensive: six lanes on
# that same machine lost an hour the night before by reading a working box as frozen. The lines
# of one run share a message, and no line names a condition other than its own.
# The two kernel stalls lead: either one means the box needs a reset, which no other line asks.
KINDS = ("writeback", "umount", "cpu", "swap", "memory")
STALL_KINDS = ("writeback", "umount")
HEADLINE = {
    "writeback": "WRITEBACK SWITCH STALL",
    "umount": "UMOUNT HUNG",
    "cpu": "BOX SHORT OF CPU",
    "swap": "BOX OUT OF SWAP",
    "memory": "BOX SHORT OF MEMORY",
}
STILL = {
    "writeback": "STILL A WRITEBACK SWITCH STALL",
    "umount": "STILL UMOUNT HUNG",
    "cpu": "STILL SHORT OF CPU",
    "swap": "STILL OUT OF SWAP",
    "memory": "STILL SHORT OF MEMORY",
}


def mem_floor(now: dict, options: argparse.Namespace) -> tuple[float, str]:
    """(the MemAvailable floor for THIS box, how it was arrived at).

    A single absolute number cannot serve every box this watch runs on. `--mem-floor` 8 GiB is
    12.5% of a 64 GB lane VM, 3.2% of the 251 GB devbox and 0.5% of a 1.5 TB builder: on the
    big boxes it is reached only once the box is already dying. So the floor is at least
    `--mem-floor-fraction` of MemTotal.

    And it is never more than `--mem-floor-cap` of MemTotal. A floor above a quarter of the box
    is not a floor, it is a permanent alert: on 2026-10-31 at 03:49:22Z a hand run with
    `--mem-floor 200000000000` fired on ossv-agent-repo, a 64 GB box, saying "MemAvailable is
    58.6 GiB, under the 186.3 GiB floor". The cap makes a mis-set floor harmless instead of a
    false alarm every period.

    A box whose MemTotal cannot be read keeps the absolute floor: the fraction has nothing to
    be a fraction of, and the cap must not then clamp the floor to zero and silence the rule.
    """
    total = now.get("mem_total") or 0
    if not total:
        return options.mem_floor, f"{gib(options.mem_floor)} absolute; MemTotal unreadable"
    scaled = options.mem_floor_fraction * total
    floor = max(options.mem_floor, scaled)
    source = (
        f"{gib(options.mem_floor)} absolute"
        if options.mem_floor >= scaled
        else f"{100 * options.mem_floor_fraction:.0f}% of this box's {gib(total)}"
    )
    cap = options.mem_floor_cap * total
    if floor > cap:
        return cap, (
            f"{source} capped at {100 * options.mem_floor_cap:.0f}% of this box's {gib(total)}"
        )
    return floor, source


def cpu_finding(now: dict, options: argparse.Namespace) -> dict | None:
    """Whether this box is short of CPU, and why, or None.

    Two gates, because either on its own is wrong here. PSI alone called ossv-agent-open starved
    at 7%; the run queue alone counts uninterruptible sleep and so cannot tell a box short of CPU
    from one waiting on a disk. A load at or under the core count is a box whose work fits on its
    cores, whatever its pressure reads.
    """
    cores = now["cores"]
    cpu_some = now["cpu"].get("some_avg60")
    load = f"load {now['load1']:.0f}/{now['load5']:.0f}/{now['load15']:.0f} on {cores} cores"
    queue = f"{now['procs_running']} runnable, {now['procs_blocked']} blocked"
    if cpu_some is not None:
        if cpu_some < options.cpu_stall or now["load1"] <= cores:
            return None
        return {
            "reasons": [
                f"cpu pressure some avg60 is {cpu_some:.0f}%, at or over the"
                f" {options.cpu_stall:.0f}% rule, with load {now['load1']:.0f} over"
                f" {cores} cores: that share of the last minute had a task runnable and"
                f" waiting for a CPU, on a box carrying more than it has cores"
            ],
            "evidence": f"cpu some avg60 {cpu_some:.0f}% (rule {options.cpu_stall:.0f}%) at {load}; {queue}",
        }
    # A kernel without PSI. The run queue alone is a coarser instrument, so it is the fallback
    # and never the primary: it says how many want a CPU, not how long any of them waited.
    if now["procs_running"] < options.queue_per_core * cores:
        return None
    return {
        "reasons": [
            f"no PSI on this kernel; {now['procs_running']} runnable processes on {cores}"
            f" cores, at or over the {options.queue_per_core:.1f} per core fallback rule"
        ],
        "evidence": f"no PSI here; {queue} (rule {options.queue_per_core:.1f} runnable per core) at {load}",
    }


def short_mount(mount: str, width: int = 60) -> str:
    """A mount point cut for a message line; the report names it whole."""
    return mount if len(mount) <= width else mount[: width - 1] + "…"


def worker_phrase(worker: dict, pid: bool = True) -> str:
    cpu = worker.get("cpu")
    named = f"{worker['comm']} (pid {worker['pid']})" if pid else worker["comm"]
    return f"{named}{f' at {100 * cpu:.0f}% CPU' if cpu is not None else ''}"


def writeback_finding(now: dict, options: argparse.Namespace) -> dict | None:
    """Whether inodes are being moved between cgroup writebacks the way the stall moves them, or
    None.

    Two things in the same run, because the rate alone is wrong here. Healthy boxes switch inodes
    in bursts: sami-rocqperf's 10 s counts on 2026-11-03 ran 0.7-778/s (mean 52/s, 24 of 81 at or
    over 20/s), sami-devbox's after its reset 0-867/s. What a burst never does is keep a switch
    worker busy: one was busy in 7 of 60 and 1 of 60 one-second samples on sami-rocqperf, and in
    60 of 60 on sami-devbox during the stall. So a reading counts only with a
    `+inode_switch_wbs` worker busy at the run, and the grace window (--grace, 600 s) asks it of
    every run for ten minutes. None when the rate was not read.
    """
    reading = now.get("writeback") or {}
    rate = reading.get("rate")
    if rate is None or rate < options.switch_rate or not now.get("switch_workers"):
        return None
    workers = sorted(reading.get("workers") or [], key=lambda worker: -(worker.get("cpu") or 0))
    busiest = f"; {worker_phrase(workers[0], pid=False)}" if workers else ""
    queued = reading.get("queued")
    reasons = [
        f"writeback:inode_switch_wbs counted {reading['switched']} inodes switched to another"
        f" cgroup's writeback in {reading['seconds']:g} s ({rate:.0f}/s), at or over the"
        f" {options.switch_rate:g}/s rule"
        + (f"; writeback:inode_switch_wbs_queue counted {queued} in the same interval" if queued is not None else "")
    ]
    if workers:
        reasons.append("busy switch workers: " + ", ".join(worker_phrase(worker) for worker in workers))
    return {
        "reasons": reasons,
        "evidence": f"{rate:.0f} inodes/s switched over {reading['seconds']:g} s (rule {options.switch_rate:g}/s){busiest}",
        "figure": rate,
    }


def umount_finding(now: dict, options: argparse.Namespace) -> dict | None:
    """Whether an umount or fusermount3 has been in D state past --umount-hung, or None.

    The time is already a hold, so this condition has no grace window of its own (run_once).
    """
    hung = [task for task in now.get("umounts") or [] if task["blocked"] >= options.umount_hung]
    if not hung:
        return None

    # The message names the longest-blocked task, with its mount point cut to fit; the report
    # names every one whole.
    longest = hung[0]
    shown = (
        f"pid {longest['pid']} {longest['comm']} {short_mount(longest['mount'])}"
        f" blocked {human_time(longest['blocked'])}"
    )
    more = f"; {len(hung) - 1} more in the report" if len(hung) > 1 else ""
    return {
        "reasons": [
            f"{len(hung)} umount/fusermount3 in D state for over {human_time(options.umount_hung)}"
            f" (time since each last ran, /proc/<pid>/sched): "
            + "; ".join(f"pid {task['pid']} {task['comm']} {task['mount']} blocked {human_time(task['blocked'])}" for task in hung)
        ],
        "evidence": f"{len(hung)} in D state over {human_time(options.umount_hung)}, longest {shown}{more}",
        "figure": len(hung),
        "tasks": hung,
    }


def verdict(now: dict, samples: list[list[float]], options: argparse.Namespace) -> dict:
    """What this box is short of, condition by condition, each with its own reasons."""
    findings: dict[str, dict] = {}

    # The kernel stalls. Each is judged only when its reading exists in this run.
    for kind, judge in (("writeback", writeback_finding), ("umount", umount_finding)):
        found = judge(now, options)
        if found:
            findings[kind] = found

    # A host whose CPU is meant to be full (HOST_SETTINGS, --cpu-rule off) has no CPU rule at all.
    if options.cpu_rule:
        found = cpu_finding(now, options)
        if found:
            findings["cpu"] = found

    drain, drain_window = swap_drain_rate(samples, options.rate_window)
    time_to_exhaustion = None
    if drain and drain > 0 and now["swap_free"] > 0:
        time_to_exhaustion = now["swap_free"] / drain
    # The swap rule asks nothing about CPU. Swap with nothing left is a statement about the next
    # memory squeeze, not about this minute, and it is worth saying on a box that is otherwise
    # idle: from there the kernel's next reclaim has nowhere to go but the OOM killer.
    if now["swap_total"]:
        if now["swap_used_fraction"] >= options.swap_used:
            findings["swap"] = {
                "reasons": [
                    f"swap is {100 * now['swap_used_fraction']:.1f}% used ({gib(now['swap_free'])}"
                    f" free of {gib(now['swap_total'])}), at or over the"
                    f" {100 * options.swap_used:.0f}% rule"
                ],
                "evidence": (
                    f"{100 * now['swap_used_fraction']:.1f}% used, {gib(now['swap_free'])} free of"
                    f" {gib(now['swap_total'])} (rule {100 * options.swap_used:.0f}%)"
                ),
            }
        elif time_to_exhaustion is not None and time_to_exhaustion < options.horizon:
            findings["swap"] = {
                "reasons": [
                    f"swap is draining at {gib(drain * 3600)}/h over the last"
                    f" {human_time(drain_window)}, {human_time(time_to_exhaustion)} from exhaustion"
                ],
                "evidence": (
                    f"draining {gib(drain * 3600)}/h, {human_time(time_to_exhaustion)} to exhaustion;"
                    f" {gib(now['swap_free'])} free of {gib(now['swap_total'])}"
                ),
            }

    floor, how = mem_floor(now, options)
    if now["mem_available"] and now["mem_available"] < floor:
        findings["memory"] = {
            "reasons": [
                f"MemAvailable is {gib(now['mem_available'])}, under the"
                f" {gib(floor)} floor ({how})"
            ],
            "evidence": f"MemAvailable {gib(now['mem_available'])} under the {gib(floor)} floor",
        }

    return {
        "crossing": bool(findings),
        "findings": findings,
        "kinds": sorted(findings),
        "reasons": [reason for found in findings.values() for reason in found["reasons"]],
        "time_to_exhaustion": time_to_exhaustion,
        "swap_drain": drain,
        "swap_drain_window": drain_window,
    }


def cpu_clear_held(now: dict, options: argparse.Namespace) -> str:
    """Why a CPU crossing may not clear in this run, or "" when nothing holds it.

    `cpu some` counts runnable tasks only. Tasks stuck on a disk are neither runnable nor done, so
    a stalled box reads as a box with CPU to spare: the clear of 2026-11-02T10:25Z said so at load
    851 on 64 cores with 723 tasks blocked. The crossing stays open until the stall is over and
    the CPU reading means something again.

    Nothing holds it once the load fits on the cores. That reading is not in doubt -- the box is
    carrying less work than it has cores, whatever its disk is doing -- and without this a box on
    a busy disk would carry a CPU crossing it could never close.
    """
    if now["load1"] <= now["cores"]:
        return ""
    if now["procs_blocked"] > now["cores"]:
        return f"{now['procs_blocked']} tasks blocked, more than the {now['cores']} cores"
    io = now["io"].get("some_avg60")
    if io is not None and io >= options.io_hold:
        return f"io some avg60 {io:.0f}%, at or over the {options.io_hold:.0f}% hold"
    return ""


def clears(kind: str, now: dict, options: argparse.Namespace) -> bool:
    """Hysteresis, per condition: it clears well clear of what opened it, never on the rule. A
    CPU crossing also never clears while the box is stalled (cpu_clear_held). A writeback crossing
    never clears on a rate this run could not read."""
    if kind == "writeback":
        rate = (now.get("writeback") or {}).get("rate")
        # A burst ends with its worker idle; the stall keeps one busy (writeback_finding).
        return rate is not None and (rate < options.switch_rate * options.clear_factor or not now.get("switch_workers"))
    if kind == "umount":
        return not [task for task in now.get("umounts") or [] if task["blocked"] >= options.umount_hung * options.clear_factor]
    if kind == "cpu":
        if cpu_clear_held(now, options):
            return False
        cpu_some = now["cpu"].get("some_avg60")
        if cpu_some is None:
            return now["procs_running"] < options.queue_per_core * now["cores"] * options.clear_factor
        return cpu_some < options.cpu_stall * options.clear_factor or now["load1"] <= now["cores"]
    if kind == "swap":
        return not now["swap_total"] or now["swap_used_fraction"] < options.swap_used * options.clear_factor
    if kind == "memory":
        return not now["mem_available"] or now["mem_available"] >= mem_floor(now, options)[0] / options.clear_factor
    return True


def state_line(now: dict) -> str:
    cpu = now["cpu"].get("some_avg60")
    io = now["io"].get("some_avg60")
    memory = now["memory_pressure"].get("full_avg60")
    pieces = [
        f"load {now['load1']:.0f}/{now['load5']:.0f}/{now['load15']:.0f} on {now['cores']} cores",
        f"{now['procs_running']} runnable, {now['procs_blocked']} blocked",
    ]
    if cpu is not None:
        pieces.append(f"cpu some avg60 {cpu:.0f}%")
    if io is not None:
        pieces.append(f"io some avg60 {io:.0f}%")
    if memory is not None:
        pieces.append(f"memory full avg60 {memory:.1f}%")
    if now["swap_total"]:
        pieces.append(
            f"swap {100 * now['swap_used_fraction']:.1f}% used ({gib(now['swap_free'])} free)"
        )
    pieces.append(f"MemAvailable {gib(now['mem_available'])}")
    rate = (now.get("writeback") or {}).get("rate")
    if rate is not None:
        pieces.append(f"inode_switch_wbs {rate:.0f}/s")
    if now.get("switch_workers"):
        pieces.append(f"{len(now['switch_workers'])} busy {SWITCH_WORKER} worker(s)")
    if now.get("umounts"):
        pieces.append(
            f"{len(now['umounts'])} umount/fusermount3 in D state, longest {human_time(now['umounts'][0]['blocked'])}"
        )
    return ", ".join(pieces)


def what_it_means(kind: str) -> list[str]:
    """What this condition is, and what to do about it. One condition, one action."""
    if kind in STALL_KINDS:
        return [
            "What this is: the kernel is moving the inodes of a dead cgroup to its parent's"
            " writeback, and on this 7.0 kernel (no torvalds f6988c90671e, in v7.3)"
            " cleanup_offline_cgwb re-walks every inode it already queued on each pass, so the"
            " move runs at 1-200 inodes/s and slows as it goes. Until it ends, every umount and"
            " fusermount3 waits in cgroup_writeback_umount (D state, for hours), agent boxes"
            " cannot stop or start (`agentbox new` fails at the sysbox-fs pre-register) and"
            " sync() hangs. It does not drain on its own in any useful time: on sami-devbox on"
            " 2026-11-03 it ran for over an hour and the backlog per pass grew.",
            f"DO: reset the box by the sami-devbox reset plan ({RESET_PLAN}): safe-point asks to"
            " each session, `sync -f` of each live file system, then the cloud reset. Never"
            " `systemctl reboot`, `shutdown` or plain `sync`: on 2026-11-02 `systemctl reboot` hung"
            " 36 min in shutdown on this same lock. Kill nothing on the box to free it. The cause"
            " is a dead cgroup that wrote millions of inodes (a bulk-copy unit): stop such a copy"
            " only with devbox/bulk-copy/pull-unit.sh stop, which reclaims the unit's memory first"
            " so the cgroup dies holding few inodes.",
        ]
    if kind == "cpu":
        return [
            "What this looks like from a pane: every agent still runs and answers, its"
            " transcript is still written, and tmux still paints -- but a turn that takes"
            " seconds takes tens of minutes, so the lane reads as frozen. Do not restart an"
            " agent on this signal: a restart costs its turn and adds another working set to"
            " the same box.",
            "DO: reduce the number of concurrent agents on this box (on a lane box,"
            " `ossv-worker list` and stop or pause the lanes that are idle or duplicated), then"
            " let the swapped working sets fault back in. Move new work to a box with free"
            " cores rather than raising the limit here.",
        ]
    if kind == "swap":
        return [
            "What this is: a statement about the NEXT memory squeeze, not about this minute."
            " The box may be answering perfectly while this is true. With no swap left the"
            " kernel's next reclaim has nowhere to put anything, so the OOM killer chooses"
            " which lane dies, and it does not choose the idle one.",
            "DO: stop the sessions on this box that have finished, by exact pid and with their"
            " owner asked first; each one hands back the swap it holds. Nothing here needs a"
            " restart of the box or of a live lane.",
        ]
    return [
        "What this is: the box is close to having no memory to give, which is the step after"
        " swap is gone. An OOM kill takes a whole session and every lane inside it.",
        "DO: stop finished sessions by exact pid, owner asked first, and move new work"
        " elsewhere until MemAvailable recovers.",
    ]


# What a condition's consumers are ranked by, as a message names them. A kernel stall has none.
CONSUMER = {"cpu": "CPU", "swap": "swap", "memory": "RSS"}

# What the box-health owner does, per condition, as phrases one message can join without
# repeating itself. The long form, with the reasons, is what_it_means in the report.
ACTION = {
    "cpu": ("cut the concurrent agents here", "move new work to another box", "restart no agent"),
    "swap": ("stop finished sessions by exact pid, owner asked first",),
    "memory": ("stop finished sessions by exact pid, owner asked first", "move new work to another box"),
}

# A kernel stall's action is one line that every message about it carries whole (compose_message):
# the reset, the one way not to reset, and the cause, so the next copy does not do it again.
STALL_ACTION = (
    f"DO (box-health owner): reset by the reset plan {RESET_PLAN}; never systemctl reboot (it hangs"
    " on this lock). Cause: a dead cgroup that wrote millions of inodes; stop such a copy only with"
    " devbox/bulk-copy/pull-unit.sh stop."
)


def told_of(now: dict, when: float, told: dict | None = None, figure: float | None = None) -> dict:
    """The marks a new fact must beat: the worst figure this crossing has said, per figure.

    They ratchet, and never fall back to the current reading when a message goes for some other
    reason. On 2026-11-02 sami-rocqperf said "369 blocked" at 00:20Z; at 08:10Z its load doubled
    while 67 were blocked; at 09:16Z 182 blocked doubled that 67 and went out, though the box had
    been twice as bad nine hours earlier and the reader had been told. A kernel stall's own figure
    (its switch rate, or its count of hung umounts) ratchets the same way, upwards.
    """
    told = dict(told or {})
    for key, pick in (("load1", max), ("procs_blocked", max), ("swap_free", min), ("mem_available", min)):
        told[key] = pick(now[key], told[key]) if key in told else now[key]
    if figure is not None:
        told["figure"] = max(figure, told.get("figure") or 0)
    told["at"] = when
    return told


def worse_facts(kind: str, now: dict, told: dict, finding: dict | None = None) -> list[str]:
    """What is clearly worse for this condition than the worst already said, or [] (see WORSE).

    A kernel stall is worse when its own figure doubled past the highest it read while open (the
    switch rate, or the number of umounts hung; run_once passes that mark in `told`). How long a
    task has been blocked is not a fact here: that is time passing."""
    facts = []
    if kind in STALL_KINDS:
        figure, was = (finding or {}).get("figure"), told.get("figure")
        if figure is not None and was and figure >= WORSE * was:
            if kind == "writeback":
                facts.append(f"{figure:.0f} inodes/s switched (was {was:.0f})")
            else:
                facts.append(f"{figure} umount/fusermount3 hung (was {was})")
    if kind == "cpu":
        if told["load1"] and now["load1"] >= WORSE * told["load1"]:
            facts.append(f"load {now['load1']:.0f} (was {told['load1']:.0f})")
        if now["procs_blocked"] > now["cores"] and now["procs_blocked"] >= WORSE * told["procs_blocked"]:
            facts.append(f"{now['procs_blocked']} blocked (was {told['procs_blocked']})")
    elif kind == "swap":
        was = told["swap_free"]
        if now["swap_free"] < was / WORSE and was - now["swap_free"] >= 0.01 * now["swap_total"]:
            facts.append(f"swap free {gib(now['swap_free'])} (was {gib(was)})")
    elif kind == "memory":
        was = told["mem_available"]
        if now["mem_available"] < was / WORSE and was - now["mem_available"] >= 0.01 * now["mem_total"]:
            facts.append(f"MemAvailable {gib(now['mem_available'])} (was {gib(was)})")
    return facts


def top_of(measured: dict | None, kind: str) -> tuple[str, float, int] | None:
    """The command that tops this condition's consumers: (name, total, processes), or None."""
    ranked = ((measured or {}).get("ranked") or {}).get(kind) or []
    return ranked[0] if ranked else None


def top_phrase(kind: str, top: tuple[str, float, int]) -> str:
    name, total, count = top
    return f"{name} {f'{total:.1f} cores' if kind == 'cpu' else gib(total)} ({count} procs)"


def item_line(item: dict, now: dict, options: argparse.Namespace, host: str, when: float) -> str:
    """One condition's verdict line: its own headline and only its own evidence.

    A merged message is safe only because of this: no line names two conditions, and each line
    says what is true of its condition in this run, so it cannot repeat the "STILL OUT OF
    CPU+SWAP" of 2026-10-30T01:32Z about a box whose CPU had recovered.
    """
    kind, event, incident = item["kind"], item["event"], item["incident"]
    where = f" on {host}" if host else ""
    if event == "alert":
        return f"{HEADLINE[kind]}{where}: {item['finding']['evidence']}."
    if event == "worse":
        after = human_time(when - incident["alerted_at"])
        return f"{STILL[kind]}, WORSE{where}, {after} after the alert: {'; '.join(item['facts'])}."
    if kind == "cpu":
        back = (
            f"load {now['load1']:.0f}/{now['load5']:.0f}/{now['load15']:.0f} on {now['cores']} cores,"
            f" {now['procs_blocked']} blocked"
        )
        for label, value in (("cpu some", now["cpu"].get("some_avg60")), ("io some", now["io"].get("some_avg60"))):
            if value is not None:
                back += f", {label} {value:.0f}%"
    elif kind == "swap":
        back = f"{100 * now['swap_used_fraction']:.1f}% used ({gib(now['swap_free'])} free)"
    elif kind == "memory":
        back = f"MemAvailable {gib(now['mem_available'])} (floor {gib(mem_floor(now, options)[0])})"
    elif kind == "writeback":
        back = f"inode_switch_wbs {(now.get('writeback') or {}).get('rate') or 0:.0f}/s"
    else:
        back = f"no umount or fusermount3 in D state for {human_time(options.umount_hung * options.clear_factor)}"
    opened = incident.get("opened_at") or when
    return f"{HEADLINE[kind]} CLEARED{where} after {human_time(when - opened)}: {back}."


def context_line(now: dict, kinds: list[str], measured: dict | None) -> str:
    """The pressures no verdict line printed, and the top consumer of each condition that spoke."""
    pieces = []
    cpu = now["cpu"].get("some_avg60")
    if cpu is not None and "cpu" not in kinds:
        pieces.append(f"cpu some {cpu:.0f}%")
    io = now["io"].get("some_avg60")
    if io is not None:
        pieces.append(f"io some {io:.0f}%")
    memory = now["memory_pressure"].get("full_avg60")
    if memory is not None:
        pieces.append(f"memory full {memory:.1f}%")
    for kind in kinds:
        top = top_of(measured, kind) if kind in CONSUMER else None
        if top:
            pieces.append(f"top {CONSUMER[kind]} {top_phrase(kind, top)}")
    return f"Also: {', '.join(pieces)}." if pieces else ""


def fit(lines: list[str], optional: list[str], footer: str, keep: list[str] | None = None) -> str:
    """Join a message under MESSAGE_LIMIT: drop optional lines from the end, then cut the rest.
    The footer always survives, so every message names its report, and so do the `keep` lines
    (a kernel stall's action), which go after the verdicts and before the optional lines."""
    keep = [line for line in keep or [] if line]
    optional = [line for line in optional if line]
    while optional and len("\n".join(lines + keep + optional + [footer])) > MESSAGE_LIMIT:
        optional.pop()
    tail = "\n".join(keep + optional + [footer])
    body = "\n".join(lines)
    room = max(MESSAGE_LIMIT - len(tail) - 1, 1)
    if len(body) > room:
        body = body[: room - 1] + "…"
    return f"{body}\n{tail}"


def compose_message(
    items: list[dict],
    now: dict,
    options: argparse.Namespace,
    measured: dict | None,
    report: str,
    when: float,
    host: str,
    capture: dict | None = None,
) -> str:
    """The one Envoy message of a run. Its first line is the worst verdict, which carries the
    decision: what, where, how bad. Any other verdict follows on its own line, then who acts and
    what they do, then the context; the rest is in the report."""
    # A new shortage leads, then one that worsened, then a clear: the reader sees the thing to act
    # on in the first 200 characters, which is all a notification shows.
    items = sorted(items, key=lambda item: (("alert", "worse", "clear").index(item["event"]), KINDS.index(item["kind"])))
    # The host is named once, on that first line; every line below it is about the same box.
    lines = [item_line(items[0], now, options, host, when)]
    lines += [item_line(item, now, options, "", when) for item in items[1:]]
    speaking = [item["kind"] for item in items if item["event"] != "clear"]
    optional = []
    keep = []
    if speaking:
        # A kernel stall's action is whole in every message about it; the other actions and the
        # context give way first when the message is long.
        if any(kind in STALL_KINDS for kind in speaking):
            keep = [STALL_ACTION]
            if capture:
                # The one line that says the evidence exists, as short as it can be: the reading
                # is the reader's whole question (reboot or not), and what it costs is a little
                # of the verdict line's detail, which the report holds whole.
                keep.append(f"Stack evidence in the report: {HALF_SHORT[capture['half']]}.")
        actions = dict.fromkeys(phrase for kind in speaking for phrase in ACTION.get(kind, ()))
        if actions:
            optional.append(f"DO (box-health owner): {'; '.join(actions)}.")
        optional.append(context_line(now, speaking, measured))
    tail = f"Full report: {report}" if report else "Full report: could not be written (see the journal)"
    return fit(lines, optional, tail, keep)


def report_text(
    items: list[dict],
    now: dict,
    options: argparse.Namespace,
    consumers: str,
    when: float,
    host: str,
    capture: dict | None = None,
) -> str:
    """Everything the message leaves out: the full reasons, the history, the consumer tables and
    the stack evidence of which half of the kernel stall this is (stack_section)."""
    lines = [f"load-swap-watch on {host} at {iso(when)}", f"Box: {state_line(now)}."]
    cpu = now["cpu"].get("some_avg60")
    io = now["io"].get("some_avg60")
    memory = now["memory_pressure"].get("full_avg60")
    speaking = {item["kind"] for item in items if item["event"] != "clear"}
    if "cpu" in speaking and None not in (cpu, io, memory):
        lines.append(
            f"Of the three pressures, cpu is {cpu:.0f}%, io {io:.0f}% and memory {memory:.1f}%:"
            f" {'a CPU shortage' if cpu >= max(io, memory) else 'not primarily a CPU shortage'}."
        )
    for item in items:
        kind, incident = item["kind"], item["incident"]
        opened = incident.get("opened_at") or when
        lines += ["", item_line(item, now, options, host, when)]
        if item["event"] == "clear":
            lines.append(
                f"That {kind} crossing opened at {iso(opened)}, cleared at {iso(when)}, and lasted"
                f" {human_time(when - opened)}. No action is needed."
            )
            continue
        lines.append("Why: " + "; ".join(item["finding"]["reasons"]) + ".")
        lines.append(f"This {kind} crossing opened at {iso(opened)}; first said at {iso(incident['alerted_at'])}.")
        if item["event"] == "worse":
            was = item["was"]
            lines.append(
                f"The last message of this crossing went at {iso(was['at'])}. The worst it had said:"
                f" load {was['load1']:.0f}, {was['procs_blocked']} blocked, {gib(was['swap_free'])} of"
                f" swap free, MemAvailable {gib(was['mem_available'])}."
            )
        lines += what_it_means(kind)
    if consumers:
        lines += ["", consumers]
    if capture:
        lines += ["", stack_section(capture)]
    lines += [
        "",
        "A crossing still open is said again only on a new fact, measured against the worst this"
        " crossing has already said: load, or tasks blocked past the core count, doubled; swap free"
        " or MemAvailable halved; a writeback switch rate or a count of hung umounts doubled; or a"
        f" command newly at the top of its consumers (looked for every {human_time(options.recheck)})."
        " Never because time passed.",
        "This watch only detects. It kills nothing, stops no worker, and changes no limit."
        f" From load-swap-watch (~/.dotfiles/scripts/load-swap-watch) on {host}, a systemd --user"
        " timer. Now: `load-swap-watch --consumers`. Every run: `journalctl --user -u load-swap-watch`.",
    ]
    return "\n".join(lines) + "\n"


def stall_episode(items: list[dict]) -> str:
    """The episode key of the kernel stall this run is speaking about, or "" when it speaks of
    none: the kind and opening time of the OLDEST open stall crossing, so a stall that starts as
    a hung umount and grows a writeback crossing half an hour later is still one episode and
    gets one capture."""
    opened = [
        (item["incident"].get("opened_at") or 0.0, item["kind"])
        for item in items
        if item["event"] != "clear" and item["kind"] in STALL_KINDS
    ]
    if not opened:
        return ""
    at, kind = min(opened)
    return f"{kind}:{at:.0f}"


def prune_reports(directory: Path, when: float) -> None:
    """Drop reports older than REPORT_DAYS, in the run that writes a new one."""
    for old in directory.glob("*.txt"):
        try:
            if when - old.stat().st_mtime > REPORT_DAYS * 86400:
                old.unlink()
        except OSError as error:
            log(f"  report {old} could not be pruned: {error}")


def write_report(directory: Path, label: str, when: float, body: str) -> str:
    """Write one report under `directory` and return its path, or "" when it cannot be.

    The message carries the decision and this carries everything behind it. A report that cannot
    be written (a full disk is one reason a box is in trouble) never stops the message: the
    message goes, and says the report is missing.
    """
    stamp = time.strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ", time.gmtime(when))
    try:
        directory.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        prune_reports(directory, when)
        path = directory / f"{stamp}-{label}.txt"
        count = 1
        while path.exists():  # two runs in one second: a hand run beside the timer's
            path = directory / f"{stamp}-{label}-{count}.txt"
            count += 1
        path.write_text(body.rstrip("\n") + "\n")
    except OSError as error:
        log(f"  report for {label} could not be written: {error}")
        return ""
    return str(path)


# A machine whose box health belongs to one session, not to this box's role holder, sets
# BOX_HEALTH_OWNER to that session id: the OSS Verify lane VMs run these watches from their own
# dotfiles and their alerts go to the fleet monitoring owner.
OWNER_RE = re.compile(r"01[0-9a-f]{6}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12}\Z")


def box_health_owner() -> str:
    """The session id BOX_HEALTH_OWNER names, or "" for the role-then-SRE default.

    An unusable value is refused at start, not at the first crossing: a watch that drops its
    alerts quietly is worse than one that does not run.
    """
    owner = (os.environ.get("BOX_HEALTH_OWNER") or "").strip()
    if owner and not OWNER_RE.fullmatch(owner):
        raise SystemExit(
            f"BOX_HEALTH_OWNER={owner!r} is not an omp session id"
            " (01xxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx); unset it for the box-health role"
        )
    return owner


def tell_box_health(envoy: Envoy, text: str) -> str:
    """Tell this box's health owner, and say who heard it.

    With BOX_HEALTH_OWNER set, that session is the only recipient and nothing is published to
    the role. Otherwise the role is the address while somebody holds it; an unheld role answers
    non-200, and the SRE session hears it instead.
    """
    owner = box_health_owner()
    if owner:
        return owner if envoy.send(owner, text) else ""
    if envoy.publish(BOX_HEALTH_ROLE_TOPIC, text):
        return BOX_HEALTH_ROLE_TOPIC
    if envoy.send(SRE, text):
        return SRE
    return ""


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- one run


def run_once(options: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    when = time.time()
    envoy = Envoy(timeout=options.http_timeout)
    state = State(options.state)
    if not state.locked:
        log(f"another run holds {options.state}; skipping this period")
        return 0

    now = sample_box()
    # The writeback switch rate costs a `perf stat` run, so it is read only when it can matter: a
    # switch worker is busy now, or a writeback crossing is already open, which then needs a
    # reading every run to hold, alert or clear.
    if now.get("switch_workers") or "writeback" in (state.data.get("incidents") or {}):
        now["writeback"] = switch_rate(options.switch_sample, now.get("switch_workers") or [])
        if now["writeback"]["error"]:
            log(f"writeback switch rate not read: {now['writeback']['error']}")
    samples: list = state.data.setdefault("samples", [])
    samples.append([now["at"], now["swap_free"]])
    cutoff = now["at"] - max(options.rate_window * 3, 7200)
    state.data["samples"] = [sample for sample in samples if sample[0] >= cutoff][-512:]
    state.data["last_run"] = now["at"]
    state.data["last_state"] = state_line(now)

    found = verdict(now, state.data["samples"], options)
    log(state_line(now))

    # The one change this watch makes to the box, after the reading it alerts on is taken and
    # said, so an alert never describes a box the reclaim has already changed.
    reclaim_dying(now, options, state.data)

    # One incident per condition. They open, alert, re-send and clear on their own rules, so a CPU
    # shortage that recovered can never be carried into a swap line half an hour later.
    incidents: dict = state.data.setdefault("incidents", {})
    # The single-incident state of the first version: keep nothing from it but its timestamps,
    # so an upgrade neither re-alerts what was already said nor loses an open crossing.
    legacy = state.data.pop("incident", None)
    if legacy and legacy.get("opened_at"):
        for kind in legacy.get("kinds") or []:
            incidents.setdefault(kind, dict(legacy, kinds=[kind]))
    if not options.cpu_rule and incidents.pop("cpu", None):
        # The rule was turned off for this host while a crossing was open: nobody needs a clear
        # for a rule that no longer applies here.
        log("the cpu rule is off on this host; its open crossing is dropped without a message")

    measured: dict | None = None

    def measured_once() -> dict:
        # The CPU sample and the /proc walk happen at most once per run, however many conditions
        # need them.
        nonlocal measured
        if measured is None:
            measured = measure_consumers(options)
        return measured

    items: list[dict] = []
    for kind in KINDS:
        finding = found["findings"].get(kind)
        incident = incidents.get(kind)
        if finding:
            if not incident:
                incident = incidents[kind] = {"opened_at": when, "alerted_at": None}
                log(f"{kind} crossing opened: {'; '.join(finding['reasons'])}")
            incident["last_seen"] = when
            # A kernel stall's figure swings inside the stall (57-200/s over the same hour), so
            # its new fact is measured against the highest it read while open, said or not.
            peak = incident.get("peak")
            if finding.get("figure") is not None:
                incident["peak"] = max(peak or 0, finding["figure"])
            if incident.get("alerted_at") is None:
                held = when - incident["opened_at"]
                # A hung umount's own blocked time is its hold, so it has no grace on top.
                grace = 0.0 if kind == "umount" else options.grace
                if held >= grace:
                    items.append({"kind": kind, "event": "alert", "finding": finding, "incident": incident})
                else:
                    log(f"{kind} crossing open for {human_time(held)}; alert at {iso(incident['opened_at'] + grace)}")
                continue
            told = incident.get("told")
            if not told:
                # Alerted by the version that re-sent on a timer: what it said is not on record, so
                # this reading becomes the baseline and nothing goes out for it.
                incident["told"] = told_of(now, when, figure=finding.get("figure"))
                incident["checked_at"] = when
                incident.setdefault("tops", [])
                incident.pop("escalated_at", None)
                log(f"{kind} crossing alerted at {iso(incident['alerted_at'])} has no baseline; this run's reading is it, nothing sent")
                continue
            marks = dict(told, figure=max(told.get("figure") or 0, peak or 0)) if peak is not None else told
            facts = worse_facts(kind, now, marks, finding)
            if kind in CONSUMER and when - max(told["at"], incident.get("checked_at") or 0) >= options.recheck:
                incident["checked_at"] = when
                top = top_of(measured_once(), kind)
                tops = incident.setdefault("tops", [])
                if top and top[0] not in tops:
                    was = tops[-1] if tops else "none named"
                    facts.append(f"new top {CONSUMER[kind]} consumer {top_phrase(kind, top)}, was {was}")
            if facts:
                items.append(
                    {"kind": kind, "event": "worse", "finding": finding, "incident": incident, "facts": facts, "was": dict(told)}
                )
            else:
                log(
                    f"{kind} crossing open for {human_time(when - incident['opened_at'])}; alerted at"
                    f" {iso(incident['alerted_at'])}, last said at {iso(told['at'])}; no new fact"
                )
        elif incident:
            held_by = cpu_clear_held(now, options) if kind == "cpu" else ""
            if clears(kind, now, options):
                if incident.get("alerted_at"):
                    items.append({"kind": kind, "event": "clear", "incident": incident})
                log(f"{kind} crossing cleared after {human_time(when - (incident.get('opened_at') or when))}")
                incidents.pop(kind, None)
            else:
                held_by = f"; the clear is held: {held_by}" if held_by else ""
                log(f"{kind} is under its rule but not yet clear of it{held_by}; the crossing stays open")
                incident["last_seen"] = when

    if not items:
        state.commit(options)
        state.close()
        return 0

    host = socket.gethostname()
    speaking = [item for item in items if item["event"] != "clear"]
    consumers = ""
    # A kernel stall has no consumer table: the 10 s CPU sample is paid only for a condition that
    # names its top consumer.
    if speaking:
        if any(item["kind"] in CONSUMER for item in speaking):
            consumers = consumers_text(options, envoy, measured_once())
        for item in speaking:
            incident = item["incident"]
            if item["event"] == "alert":
                incident["alerted_at"] = when
            incident["told"] = told_of(now, when, incident.get("told"), item["finding"].get("figure"))
            incident["checked_at"] = when
            tops = incident.setdefault("tops", [])
            top = top_of(measured, item["kind"])
            if top and top[0] not in tops:
                tops.append(top[0])

    # The evidence that says which half of the kernel bug this stall is, taken while the stall
    # is on: once per episode, and never twice in a minute, since `echo w` writes thousands of
    # kernel log lines. It goes into the same report the alert already names.
    capture = None
    episode = stall_episode(items)
    taken = state.data.get("stack_capture") or {}
    if not episode:
        pass
    elif options.no_stack_capture:
        log("stack evidence not taken: --no-stack-capture")
    elif options.dry_run:
        log(f"dry run: no stack evidence taken for episode {episode} (it would write to {SYSRQ_PATH})")
    elif taken.get("episode") == episode:
        log(f"stack evidence for episode {episode} was already taken at {iso(taken.get('at'))}: {taken.get('half')}")
    elif when - (taken.get("at") or 0) < STACK_GAP:
        log(f"stack evidence held off: the last capture was {human_time(when - taken['at'])} ago, one a minute")
    else:
        capture = capture_stacks(when)
        state.data["stack_capture"] = {"episode": episode, "at": when, "half": capture["half"]}
        log(f"stack evidence taken in {capture['seconds']:.1f} s: {HALF_SHORT[capture['half']]}")

    label = "alert" if any(item["event"] == "alert" for item in items) else "still" if speaking else "clear"
    directory = options.state.parent / "reports"
    text = report_text(items, now, options, consumers, when, host, capture)
    report = "" if options.dry_run else write_report(directory, label, when, text)
    message = compose_message(items, now, options, measured, report, when, host, capture)
    # Recorded as said before it is sent, so a run killed mid-send cannot say it twice.
    state.commit(options)
    if options.print_alert or options.dry_run:
        print(message)
    if options.dry_run:
        print(f"\nDry run: nothing written under {directory}. The report would read:\n{text}")
    else:
        # The send's own answer decides, not the attempt. A message that says it was told while
        # every route refused it is the silent failure this watch exists to stop seeing elsewhere.
        target = tell_box_health(envoy, message)
        said = ", ".join(f"{item['kind']} {item['event']}" for item in items)
        if target:
            log(f"told {target}: {said}")
        else:
            # The state already records the message as made, so this line is the only trace that
            # it reached nobody.
            log("NOBODY HEARD IT: the message was refused by every Envoy route; see above")
    state.close()
    return 0


def parse_args(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> argparse.Namespace:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    parser.add_argument(
        "--cpu-stall",
        type=float,
        default=CPU_STALL,
        help=(
            "alert when /proc/pressure/cpu some avg60 is at or over this percentage AND load is"
            f" over the core count (default {CPU_STALL:.0f})"
        ),
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--cpu-rule",
        choices=("on", "off"),
        help="judge CPU at all (default: on, except where HOST_SETTINGS turns it off for this host)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--io-hold",
        type=float,
        default=IO_HOLD,
        help=(
            "a CPU crossing does not clear while io some avg60 is at or over this percentage, nor"
            f" while more tasks are blocked than the box has cores (default {IO_HOLD:.0f})"
        ),
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--switch-rate",
        type=float,
        default=SWITCH_RATE,
        help=(
            "writeback switch stall: writeback:inode_switch_wbs events per second at or over this,"
            f" held for --grace (default {SWITCH_RATE:g})"
        ),
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--switch-sample",
        type=float,
        default=SWITCH_SAMPLE,
        help=f"seconds one `sudo -n perf stat` count of the switch rate runs for (default {SWITCH_SAMPLE:g})",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--reclaim",
        action=argparse.BooleanOptionalAction,
        default=True,
        help="reclaim bounded memory from user.slice when the dying cgroup count is over"
        " --dying-threshold, so the kernel can free those groups (default on)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--dying-threshold",
        type=int,
        default=DYING_THRESHOLD,
        help=f"reclaim only above this many dying cgroups (default {DYING_THRESHOLD})",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--reclaim-bytes",
        type=int,
        default=RECLAIM_BYTES,
        help=f"the most one run may ask user.slice to reclaim (default {RECLAIM_BYTES // GIB} GiB,"
        f" in {RECLAIM_STEP // GIB} GiB steps)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--reclaim-io-hold",
        type=float,
        default=RECLAIM_IO_HOLD,
        help="never start a reclaim, and stop one, when /proc/pressure/io full avg10 is at or"
        f" over this (default {RECLAIM_IO_HOLD:g})",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--umount-hung",
        type=float,
        default=UMOUNT_HUNG,
        help=f"an umount or fusermount3 in D state longer than this many seconds is hung (default {UMOUNT_HUNG:g})",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--queue-per-core",
        type=float,
        default=3.0,
        help="fallback rule on a kernel without PSI: runnable processes per core (default 3)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--swap-used",
        type=float,
        default=0.85,
        help="alert when this fraction of swap is in use (default 0.85)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--mem-floor",
        type=float,
        default=8 * GIB,
        help="the absolute MemAvailable floor in bytes (default 8 GiB); see --mem-floor-fraction",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--mem-floor-fraction",
        type=float,
        default=0.10,
        help="the floor is at least this fraction of MemTotal (default 0.10)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--mem-floor-cap",
        type=float,
        default=0.25,
        help="and never more than this fraction of MemTotal (default 0.25)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--horizon",
        type=float,
        default=7200.0,
        help="alert when swap is draining and reaches zero inside this many seconds (default 2 h)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--rate-window",
        type=float,
        default=3600.0,
        help="window of this watch's own samples the swap drain rate is measured over (default 1 h)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--grace",
        type=float,
        default=600.0,
        help="a crossing must hold this long before it alerts (default 600 s)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--recheck",
        type=float,
        default=1800.0,
        help=(
            "while a crossing stays open, look for a new top consumer this long after it was last"
            " said (default 30 min); it is said again only on a new fact, never on this timer alone"
        ),
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--clear-factor",
        type=float,
        default=0.7,
        help="hysteresis: a crossing clears at this fraction of the rule that opened it (default 0.7)",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--sample-seconds",
        type=float,
        default=10.0,
        help="interval the per-process CPU delta in a report is measured over (default 10 s)",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--top", type=int, default=8, help="processes named per list in a report (default 8)")
    parser.add_argument("--state", type=Path, default=DEFAULT_STATE, help=f"state file (default {DEFAULT_STATE})")
    parser.add_argument("--http-timeout", type=float, default=8.0, help="Envoy request timeout (default 8 s)")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--dry-run", action="store_true", help="decide and print the message and its report; send nothing, write no state or report"
    )
    parser.add_argument("--write-state", action="store_true", help="with --dry-run, still write the state file")
    parser.add_argument("--print-alert", action="store_true", help="print the message to stdout as well as sending it")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--consumers",
        action="store_true",
        help="print who is using the CPU and holding the swap now, and exit; decides nothing",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", help="with --consumers, print one JSON object")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--capture-stacks",
        action="store_true",
        help=(
            "take the stack evidence now and print the report section it would write, then exit;"
            f" this is the one hand-run path that writes to {SYSRQ_PATH}"
        ),
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--no-stack-capture",
        action="store_true",
        help=f"alert on a stall without taking the stack evidence (no write to {SYSRQ_PATH})",
    )
    options = parser.parse_args(argv)
    host = socket.gethostname().split(".")[0]
    options.cpu_rule = (
        HOST_SETTINGS.get(host, {}).get("cpu_rule", True) if options.cpu_rule is None else options.cpu_rule == "on"
    )
    return options


def main() -> int:
    options = parse_args()
    box_health_owner()  # refuse an unusable BOX_HEALTH_OWNER before any work

    # The hand-run proof that the capture works, and the way to take the evidence during a
    # stall when no crossing is open yet.
    if options.capture_stacks:
        print(stack_section(capture_stacks(time.time())))
        return 0

    if options.consumers:
        # The hand-run path names processes too, so it needs the same two lookups.
        refresh_panes()
        try:
            refresh_live_sessions(Envoy(timeout=options.http_timeout))
        except SystemExit as error:
            log(f"  live sessions unavailable: {error}; attribution unverified")
        if options.json:
            cpu_rows, span = sample_cpu(options.sample_seconds)
            print(
                json.dumps(
                    {
                        "box": sample_box(),
                        "census": agent_census(),
                        "cpu_interval": span,
                        "cpu": [
                            dict(row, who=describe(row["pid"])) for row in cpu_rows[: options.top]
                        ],
                        "swap": [
                            dict(row, who=describe(row["pid"]))
                            for row in [row for row in sample_memory() if row["swap"]][: options.top]
                        ],
                    },
                    indent=1,
                )
            )
        else:
            print(state_line(sample_box()))
            print(consumers_text(options))
        return 0
    return run_once(options)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
